@article{williamsRegulationChecklistCommon2010,
  title = {Regulation Checklist: Common Pitfalls in Regulations},
  shorttitle = {Regulation Checklist},
  author = {Williams, Richard},
  year = 2010,
  journal = {Mercatus Center, George Mason University},
  pages = {11},
  langid = {english},
  note = {No. 10-01
\par
January 2010
\par
working
\par
paper
\par
Regulation Checklist: Common Pitfalls in Regulations
\par
By Richard Williams
\par
The ideas presented in this research are the author's and do not represent official positions
\par
of the Mercatus Center at George Mason University.\textbf{Introduction}
\par
Federal regulations are mandated by the executive branch of the federal government and are used
\par
to implement, in some cases to finely tune, laws from Congress. They reach most aspects of
\par
American life and touch every single individual in some way. The U.S. federal government
\par
creates an average of about 4,000 final regulations each year with about 500--700 reviewed by
\par
the White House (the Office of Information and Regulatory Affairs). Of those reviewed,
\par
between 45 and 75 have significant economic impacts.i Most of the time, people that comment
\par
on regulations are those who feel directly impacted by those regulations, usually because they
\par
impose either a large cost on them or their company, lead to a loss of liberty, or because they
\par
address a problem that people feel deeply about. 1 But for most Americans and, in fact, many
\par
regulated firms (literally all firms), regulations and how they work are a mystery and they have
\par
no idea how to comment effectively. It is important that people concerned with regulations
\par
know what to look for and how to comment effectively.
\par
The process of regulating is governed by the 1946 Administrative Procedures Act (APA) which,
\par
as part of democratic government, requires agencies to solicit comments and respond to all
\par
significant comments.2 In general, comments on regulations may either be submitted
\par
electronically or in writing. Regulations are designed to interpret laws3 and may do so by further
\par
defining statutory terms, establishing a process to comply with the law, or establishing a
\par
1 A comment on a proposed federal rule, which is called a regulation when it is completed, is a written submission
\par
to the agency that is trying to put the rule forward that discusses any aspect of the proposed rule. It may be
\par
mailed to the agency or submitted as an email. At the end of each rule, the agency designates an individual with
\par
an address to which comments are directed.
\par
2 In cases where there are many comments that are the same, the agency may respond to them as a group rather
\par
than address them individually.
\par
3 Laws that direct regulatory agencies are passed by Congress and signed by the president. They tell the agency
\par
what they either can or must do in a regulation. Some are very specific and some are vague, leaving more
\par
discretion to the agency in the latter case.
\par
1standard. They have the force and effect of law and, in fact, many of the issues discussed in this
\par
paper might arise as a result of the original law. Agencies are bound by those laws and, if the
\par
issue is with the law itself, it may be more appropriate to appeal to Congress than to the agency.4
\par
There is a concern by some that regulation controls a large part of our lives and yet is carried out
\par
by unelected bureaucrats.5 Although executive branch agencies are under the direct control of
\par
the president and can be monitored by Congress, it is true that they have a great deal of
\par
autonomy. This is particularly so as so many regulations require the work of experts to
\par
accomplish them. This makes it all the more important that citizens insist that their regulations
\par
be intelligible and easy to comment upon.
\par
One of the most common ways that agencies create regulations is through \texthorizontalbar informal
\par
rulemaking.\textbardbl{} In this type of rulemaking, agencies will usually publish a proposed rule (Notice of
\par
Proposed Rulemaking or NPRM) in the \emph{Federal Register} that contains both a \texthorizontalbar codified\textbardbl{} part of
\par
the regulation (the actual rules that, when finalized, will appear in the Code of Federal
\par
Regulations) and a \texthorizontalbar preamble,\textbardbl{} which has a discussion of why and how the agency thinks the
\par
rules will accomplish the goal of the regulation. It will also solicit information and discuss the
\par
rules it proposes to codify.6 In addition, in proposed rules there will be a cost-benefit analysis
\par
(Preliminary Regulatory Impact Analysis) of the rules (particularly for economically significant
\par
regulations), which is required by executive order (from the president), and an Initial Regulatory
\par
4 People often will write or visit their congressional representatives to make their views known on a regulation.
\par
5 It was felt that the reason Congress needed to delegate such broad powers to regulate virtually all portions of the
\par
economy was that there were so many complex areas that Congress simply did not have the time or expertise to
\par
handle them.
\par
6 The APA ``affords all interested persons an adequate opportunity to provide data, views, and arguments with
\par
respect to the agency's proposals and any alternative proposals of other interested persons.'' To afford all
\par
interested persons an adequate opportunity to provide data, views, and arguments with respect to the agency's
\par
proposals and any alternative proposals of other interested persons.
\par
2Flexibility Analysis (IRFA), which is an analysis of how the regulation will affect small
\par
businesses or other small entities. 7
\par
Before putting out a proposed rule, the agency may publish an Advanced Notice of Proposed
\par
Rulemaking (ANPRM) in the \emph{Federal Register}, or it may meet with various constituencies to
\par
solicit comments on how the rule should be crafted. An ANPRM is generally published when the
\par
agency needs much more information before it chooses a course of action. For either an ANPRM
\par
or NPRM, once they have been published in the \emph{Federal Register}, anyone may send the agency a
\par
written or electronic comment. A time limit for the acceptance of comments is specified in the
\par
proposal.8 To find these regulations and comment, there is a clearinghouse for regulations at the
\par
multi-agency Web site called Regulations.gov. Regulations.gov is a part of what has been
\par
termed \texthorizontalbar e-rulemaking\textbardbl{} that lists rules, ways to comment and, eventually, will have a \texthorizontalbar docket\textbardbl{}
\par
with all other comments on those rules. There are ways in which people can be notified about
\par
recently published regulations using Regulations.gov.9 The final rule is also published in the
