\par
There is nothing inherently good or bad about regulations as a class just as might be said of the
\par
laws that underlie them. But not all regulations are necessary and, even when they are, it is
\par
possible to write them in such a way that they are not as helpful as they might be. An analogy to
\par
regulations that reduce risk might be chemicals that are put into the community pool to control
\par
the spread of illness. If you don`t put in enough chemicals, they will not be effective. If you put
\par
too much, the pool will be an unpleasant place to swim. If you put the wrong kinds of chemicals,
\par
you may cause other illnesses. Most people trust that there is an expert who knows precisely the
\par
right kind and amount of chemicals but in reality, it is probably the 17-year-old lifeguard hired
\par
for the summer. Regulatory professionals do have knowledgeable experts in charge but because
\par
of the pressures exerted on them by various constituencies, including media, they may over or
\par
under reduce risk through regulation.ii
\par
Although regulations are continually being modified, for the most part, once they are in place,
\par
they tend to stay in place as there is no real demand by any group to remove old, outdated, or
\par
unworkable regulations, even thought there are rules that are suppose to make that happen.
\par
iii
\par
Therefore, it is important that many different voices are heard in comments to proposed
\par
regulations.
\par
There are numerous people in the federal government who review rules and may be impacted by
\par
comments. For example, a rule being written in the Food Safety and Inspection Service (FSIS)
\par
4of USDA will: 1) be written by a program office; 2) will then be reviewed by managers higher
\par
up in FSIS; 3) will be reviewed by the Office of General Counsel, and the Office or Risk
\par
Assessment and Benefit-Cost Analysis (the latter if it is a health or safety rule); 4) reviewed by
\par
senior managers in the Office of the Secretary of USDA; 5) reviewed by a desk officer and an
\par
economist in the Office of Information and Regulatory Affairs in the Office of Management and
\par
Budget; 6) reviewed by an advocate at the Office of Advocacy in the Small Business
\par
Administration and; 7) possibly, reviewed by other senior White House officials all the way up
\par
to the president. Decisions may change at any point in this chain, either through direct authority
\par
(The president is the ultimate authority) or by suggestions for change. That is just the executive
\par
branch. It is also possible that the regulation may be the subject of hearings by Congress or
\par
reviewed by the courts.
\par
Participation in Rulemaking
\par
Even before creation of the APA in 1946, there was concern about ensuring that rules were
\par
produced as democratically as possible. Legislators, sometimes for different reasons, have
\par
altered or added to the rules that govern the creation of regulations to try and ensure as broad and
\par
as deep participation as possible. In order for a person or organization to successfully participate
\par
in rulemaking, they must: 1) know about a rules existence, particularly at the proposal or pre-
\par
proposal stage; 2) have access to all supporting documents and meetings; 3) be able to
\par
understand the rule, the supporting analysis of the rule and how it is likely to affect them; 4) have
\par
the right to lobby the agency or Congress to affect the rule; 5) be able to do all of these things at
\par
sufficiently low cost to make it worthwhile to do so; and finally, 6) have some possibility of
\par
actually affecting a rule in their favor.
\par
5For rulemaking to work for all stakeholders, they must:
\par
1. Know about a rules existence, particularly at the proposal or pre-proposal stage. The
\par
Unified Agenda and Regulations.gov are two government innovations that try to alert
\par
people to rules that are forthcoming or rules that have recently been proposed.iv In
\par
addition, there are requirements in the Small Business Regulatory Enforcement and
\par
Fairness Act (SBREFA) that requires OSHA and EPA to contact small businesses and
\par
solicit input from them. Privately, larger organizations either have staff devoted to
\par
tracking regulations or use third party suppliers for regulatory information. These third
\par
party suppliers focus either on industries (like trade organizations) or agencies, like
\par
Inside EPA or Food Chemical News. Traditional media, on the other hand, has
\par
historically only reported on rules that are large or controversial. In part because of the
\par
low media interest, following upcoming regulations is too costly for most individuals and
\par
very small firms. Although there were multiple outreach experiments in the 1970s, this
\par
situation still largely persists.
\par
v
\par
2. Have access to all supporting documents and meetings. The Freedom of Information Act
\par
(FOIA) of 1966 ensures that individuals have access to U.S. government records and
\par
places the burden on the government to prove that records should not be released. A
\par
FOIA request must be written however and there may be fees for supplying documents.
\par
Further, it may take some time to grant a FOIA request. However, documents that an
\par
agency has relied upon to make a regulatory finding must be available in the agencies`
\par
\texthorizontalbar Docket.\textbardbl{} In some cases, dockets are posted electronically but in others individuals must
\par
go to the agency to request supporting documentation. In addition, the Federal Advisory
\par
63. 4. Committee Act of 1972 (FACA) requires that advisory committees are objective and
\par
accessible to the public. The Government in the Sunshine Act of 1976 required that
\par
agency meetings were open to public observation although there are many exemptions.vi
\par
Be able to understand the rule, the supporting analysis of the rule and how it is likely to
\par
affect them. Rules have become both longer and more complex, particularly in the area
\par
of social regulations that are often supported by highly technical economic and risk
\par
analyses. In addition, preambles contain legal and scientific defenses that are sometimes
\par
written in highly technical language. In 1998, President Clinton issued the memorandum
\par
on Plain Language in Government Writing to help combat this.vii But trying to
\par
understand rules, their salience for particular groups, and particularly, what might be
\par
wrong with them remains a technically complex enterprise beyond many stakeholders.
\par
This paper takes one small step to help with this problem.
\par
Have the right to lobby the agencies or Congress to affect the rule. This right has always
\par
existed although it was formalized by the Administrative Procedures Act of 1946. Even
\par
as far back as the 1930s, there were multiple ways to interact with agencies, including
\par
\texthorizontalbar oral or written communication and consultation; investigations, specially summoned
\par
conferences; advisory committees and hearings.\textbardbl viii In the 1970s, many statutes required
\par
hearings with the public including the Occupational Safety and Health Act and the
\par
Consumer Product Safety Act. Although it may not have been the intention at the time,
\par
the creation of the Office of Information and Regulatory Affairs (OIRA) in OMB on
\par
April 21, 1981 offers an alternative place in the executive branch, outside of the
\par
originating agency, for stakeholders to express their concerns.
\par
75. 6. Be able to do all of these things at sufficiently low cost to make it worthwhile to do so.
\par
One rule that helps to lower costs for participation is giving participants more time to
\par
comments on rules, notably going from 30 to 60 days. For example, the 1977 amendment
\par
to the Clean Water Act required that the Agency give 60 days for comment. Another
\par
means that the federal government has made to help lower costs is electronic submission
\par
of comments on Regulations.gov.
\par
Have some possibility of actually affecting a rule in their favor. One method of ensuring
\par
that affected stakeholders have a real possibility of affecting the outcome of a rule is
\par
when they are promulgated by a little used addendum to the APA known as regulatory
\par
negotiation or \texthorizontalbar reg neg.\textbardbl{} These mechanisms are the outcome of the Negotiated
\par
Rulemaking Act of 1990 and allow for direct participation in crafting a rule. For normal
\par
rulemaking, there are multiple ways to try and influence rules: through Congressional
\par
representatives prior to, during or after rulemaking, through meetings with OIRA,
\par
meetings with the agency itself, by serving on advisory panels, through written or oral
\par
comments during public hearings or, if necessary through the courts. One thing that
\par
should be pointed out is that agencies may spend years developing a proposal and have
\par
considered a great deal of input prior to the proposal appearing in the \emph{Federal Register}
\par
and this area is not directly governed by the APA.ix This is not to say that the notice and
\par
comment period is not effective and, in fact, \texthorizontalbar over the past several decades the courts
\par
have encouraged the bureaucracy to be more responsive to those groups and individuals
\par
who voice their opinions during rulemaking.\textbardbl x
\par
8In every one of these categories, Congress or the executive branch has tried to make is
\par
possible for stakeholders to successfully participate in rulemakings.
\par
Does Participation Work?
\par
Despite all of these innovations, successful participation is not yet where it needs to be for
\par
rulemaking to be truly democratic. The procedural rules discussed above may be viewed as
\par
trying to overcome the political problems inherent in any government action. Overcoming the
\par
problems associated with a monopoly provider of a service (government regulation) with vested
\par
interests to respond to votes, money, or philosophical compatibility is always going to be
\par
extremely difficult. This will be particularly true for those that agencies view as powerless,
\par
particularly because \texthorizontalbar The Administrative Procedures Act is silent with respect to instructing
\par
agencies what to do with the comments they receive.\textbardbl xi Although they tend to ignore some,
\par
agencies do pay attention to larger, more organized groups.xii Agencies will be somewhat
\par
responsive to interest groups, and Kerwin finds that over 80 percent of interest groups surveyed
\par
believe they have influenced rulemaking over half the time they comment and half of those are
\par
influential over 75 percent of the times they comment.xiii As to why they pay attention, one
\par
author suggests that bureaucracies pay attention to interest groups that either provide new data or
\par
help protect them against future legal challenges.xiv Although past models of influence over
\par
regulatory agencies suggests permanent capture (iron triangles consisting of Congress, the
\par
bureaucracies, and interest groups) by organized interest groups, more modern theory suggests
\par
that influence is exerted by shifting groups who exert influence over \texthorizontalbar issues of interest.\textbardbl xv That
\par
is, participation is characterized by \texthorizontalbar issue networks\textbardbl{} that \texthorizontalbar are shared knowledge groups that tie
\par
together large numbers of participants with common technical expertise.\textbardbl xvi As a percentage,
\par
9business groups are the most frequent commenters on rules. The reason that advocacy groups
\par
do not submit as many comments may be because the agencies that they are interested in tend to
\par
accept advice from those who agree with their position.xvii10 For example, environmental groups
\par
interests tend to neatly coincide with the interests of the bureaucrats in EPA.xviii
\par
In sum, for those groups who are successful in influencing agencies, it is likely that they have
\par
more concentrated benefits, more resources, or tend to agree with bureaucratic preferences. For
\par
the others, there is a clear absence of \texthorizontalbar actual citizen participation\textbardbl{} in rulemaking.xix The others
\par
in this case are likely to be citizens who are not part of activists groups and small businesses.
\par
Armed with better information that can lower the cost of understanding and commenting on
\par
regulations, those currently left out may increase participation rates.11 With most of the 115
\par
million households in the United States having access to the Web through the internet and
\par
virtually all small businesses having access, there are issue networks that may be virtually
\par
formed to come together and exert influence that has previously been missing. So far, however,
\par
research has not demonstrated that this rulemaking revolution has occurred.xx This does not
\par
mean that more does not need to be done to produce more understandable regulations, better
\par
analysis on who is affected and how, and more pressure placed on agencies to defend why they
\par
have rejected individual comments. In particular, more pressure should be placed on agencies to
\par
defend dismissal of comments that disagree with proposed positions, particularly if those groups
\par
were not part of the pre-proposal stage of rulemaking. The following section is intended to help
