\par
Issues with Regulations
\par
The following is a list that describes both signals that something might be wrong with a
\par
regulation and many of the common problems with regulations that may be the subject of a
\par
comment.
\par
\textbf{A. Signals}
\par
\emph{These are not problems with regulations per se but are evidence that a deeper look is}
\par
\emph{warranted.}
\par
A.1. The regulation is designed to help some at the expense of others. The regulation may end
\par
up helping society overall but the motivation for the regulation, if it can be discerned, should
\par
make you suspicious. Firms and industries have used regulations since the beginning to raise
\par
rival`s costs. The two groups of firms that are normally adversely affected are those that are
\par
either not using a particular technology when a technology is mandated by the government or
\par
small firms who must bear large, fixed costs over a smaller sales base. This problem is
\par
compounded when there is a \texthorizontalbar bootlegger and Baptist\textbardbl{} problem where advocacy groups and a
\par
sector of industry agree on the need for regulation to suit their own purposes.xxi
\par
A.2. There is a problem that is heavily reported in the news and the regulatory agency is pressed
\par
to come up with a solution. When in this situation, regulatory agencies may be forced to appear
\par
11to be addressing the problem, even when they don`t have a solution. It should not necessarily be
\par
presumed to be a bad regulation, just that it is less likely to be a good one.
\par
A.3. The regulations are passed in the \texthorizontalbar midnight period\textbardbl{} between (presidential) election results
\par
with a new incoming president and inauguration with little review, stakeholder consent, or
\par
accountability by the executive branch.xxii Studies have shown that more regulations, and
\par
particularly more economically significant regulations, have been passed during this period.
\par
There have been many reasons offered for this phenomenon, such as trying to embarrass the next
\par
administration, trying to fill out resumes before leaving administrative posts, or perhaps trying to
\par
get politically explosive rules through that earlier would have caused problems for the incumbent
\par
administration. No matter what the reason, stakeholders should view rules passed in this period
\par
with some suspicion.
\par
A.4. The data presented has not been adequately peer reviewed. It may be that the data is not in
\par
compliance with the Information Quality Act and it may use poor or biased data.xxiii The
\par
Information Quality Act (also known as the Data Quality Act) aims to ensure the \texthorizontalbar quality,
\par
objectivity, utility, and integrity\textbardbl{} of information that agencies either disseminate or use in
\par
rulemaking. It provides a vehicle for stakeholders to request corrections and improvements to
\par
data where it falls short of meeting these goals (although responses to these requests are not
\par
judicially reviewable). A plain understanding of this act is that it attempts to keep \texthorizontalbar junk science\textbardbl{}
\par
out of rulemaking. Stakeholders should carefully examine the science that agencies use to
\par
determine whether it is credible. Publication in a peer-reviewed journal is one way this can be
\par
accomplished or the agency may put together its own peer-review process.
\par
12A.5. The agency relies heavily on inside experts for data. It is not unusual to have to rely on
\par
expert opinion used as data inputs for analyses. All such inputs should be documented with
\par
names of individuals attached to them. If all of the experts relied on work for the agency, dig
\par
deeper. Reliance on expert opinion should be robustly supported and the best way to do this is
\par
with multiple outside experts.xxiv
\par
A.6. It is clear that an agency does not understand the industry or industries it is regulating.
\par
There will often be a discussion of industry practices either in the main part of the preamble to a
\par
rule or in the regulatory impact analysis that describes the baseline against which the regulation
\par
will induce changes. An example of this was an FDA regulation of smoked fish for botulism that
\par
required cooking to such an extent that the product was inedible.xxv
\par
\textbf{Issues with Regulations}
\par
The next several sections contain issues that that any potential commenter should be aware of
\par
when looking to comment on a regulation. In general, they fall into three categories: process,
\par
analysis, and outcome. If an agency has failed to write a regulation using an appropriate process,
\par
such as is outlined in the Administrative Procedures Act and other laws, that can be an issue for
\par
courts or oversight groups such as OMB or Congress. Since the first comprehensive executive
\par
order on economic analysis passed by President Carter (Executive Order 12044), the
\par
requirements for analysis have expanded rapidly.xxvi Finally, the outcome of the regulation, what
\par
the rule actually requires, and more importantly, how markets respond to those requirements, is
\par
what actually affects Americans. Interestingly, if the agency has followed the correct process
\par
and done all of the analysis correctly (and given only pro forma responses to the commenters and
\par
ignored the results of its own analysis), there is usually no remedy by the courts, changes are
\par
13likely to come only from those that oversee regulatory agencies.xxvii In short, the courts will only
\par
concern themselves if the agency has violated a statute like the APA, a procedural statute like the
\par
Paperwork Reduction Act, or the agency`s organic statute. Violation of executive orders or
\par
\texthorizontalbar good\textbardbl{} policy are not judicially reviewable unless litigants can show that the agency has been
\par
arbitrary and capricious.
\par
\textbf{B. Need for Regulation}
\par
\emph{There is a strong incentive for regulatory agencies to regulate, after all, that is the business they}
\par
\emph{are in and their measures of success for some has been more or larger regulations. However,}
\par
\emph{there is not always a need for regulation and when there is not, the likelihood is that there will}
\par
\emph{be net social harms.}
\par
B.1. There is no problem to be solved. The bar for regulations in administrative law is fairly low
\par
and agencies are given deference in how they interpret statutes.xxviii In some cases, evidence for
\par
the need for a regulation can come from anecdotal evidence of problems that are not necessarily
\par
recurring. But regulations generally stay fixed in place forever and should only be used to fix
\par
systemic problems, problems that are expected to occur forever without a regulation. (Note: The
\par
bar is higher for an economic benefit analysis.
\par
xxix)
\par
B.2. The agency has not seriously considered issues of federalism, a basic division of
\par
governmental responsibilities established by the Constitution.xxx In addition to the constitutional
\par
question, states or localities with more local knowledge and more targeted regulations may be
\par
the appropriate place to address problems.xxxi In this case, there is no need for \emph{federal} regulation.
\par
14B.3. The market will fix itself. The baseline for regulations should be one that projects what
\par
would happen without the regulation. If the market is already moving in the right direction, and
\par
the incentives to continue to do so are strong, no regulation is needed. Note that this implies
\par
both low (or no) costs and low benefits if a regulation is passed.xxxii
\par
B.4 The regulation does not take into account other regulations that address the same problem,
\par
thus solving the problem more than once (at least analytically).
\par
xxxiii
\par
B.5. The risk is insignificant. This is somewhat related to an issue where there is no problem to
\par
be fixed but in this case, there can be a small yet persistent risks that is simply not worth fixing
\par
by federal regulation. In legal language, this concept is known as \emph{de minimis non curat lex} (the
\par
law takes no account of trifles). Unfortunately, as bureaucracies do not wish to close up shop,
\par
they must find more and more risks to regulate, even if they are small.xxxiv
\par
