\textbf{E. Economic Analysis}
\par
\emph{There are different types of analyses that agencies are required to do as part of both proposals}
\par
\emph{and final rules. Perhaps most significantly, there is an executive order that requires both a}
\par
18\emph{benefit-cost analysis and a cost-effectiveness analysis. Currently, there is guidance on how to do}
\par
\emph{economic analysis in Circular A-4 from OMB).xli There is also a statutory requirement to}
\par
\emph{analyze the effects of regulations on small entities, including small businesses.}
\par
E.1. The agency does not acknowledge the uncertainty of its findings and, even where it does so,
\par
does not evaluate how making alternative assumptions would change the outcome of their
\par
analysis. This may also be manifested as false precision. Uncertainty is always present in
\par
analyses including scientific findings, risk assessment, and benefit-cost analysis.xlii
\par
E.2. The agency fails to identify (and quantify if possible) separate costs of a regulation. One
\par
prime candidate for this is the cost to firms of management oversight of regulations. Comments
\par
from some firms show that they do not always understand this as these are not accounting costs,
\par
they are the opportunity costs of management time.13 Costs to government in administering
\par
regulations should also be included. Loss of liberty is another cost that agencies may leave
\par
out.xliii Unlike many other types of costs, attenuation of liberty may become more problematical
\par
as the rules build up. This cost has generally not been addressed in regulatory impact analyses.
\par
E.3. The agency has failed to analyze a sufficient number or variety of options. Even when
\par
agencies do manage to evaluate a sufficient number of options, they often evaluate a few options
\par
that are nonsensical or not much of a deviation from their preferred alternative. Although they
\par
may not regulate outside of their statutory authority, they should analyze options outside of their
\par
statutory authority.xliv
\par
13 Opportunity costs are the costs of allocating resources (labor and capital) away from one use to another use. So
\par
even though management would be present and there is no increase in the costs of management time
\par
(accounting) due to complying with a regulation, there is an opportunity cost as management must stop working
\par
on other matters, e.g., firm organization, looking for new business opportunities, and focus on how the firm should
\par
comply with the rule.
\par
19E.4. While some parts of the regulation may be well supported, others are not. From an
\par
economic point of view, if parts of the regulation have costs exceeding benefits, even if the
\par
overall regulation has benefits exceeding costs, then those weakly supported parts should be
\par
eliminated---unless there are other reasons (outside of benefits and costs) to keep them. In many
\par
cases, agencies will cover more firms than necessary just to \texthorizontalbar cover all of the bases\textbardbl{} and justify it
\par
as applying a \texthorizontalbar level playing field,\textbardbl{} but, in fact, it has the effect of making firms and consumers
\par
pay more for products that don`t have problems.xlv
\par
E.5. The regulation extends \texthorizontalbar benefits transfer\textbardbl{} or other types of science from one rule or study in
\par
a different context to the present one inappropriately.xlvi This may also be done inappropriately
\par
for costs if cost estimates are used for a model plant or another industry inappropriately.
\par
E.6. The regulation will severely inhibit technological growth and innovation without explicitly
\par
acknowledging this and carefully examining the trade-offs. Regulation can divert resources used
\par
for growth and innovation to other things, or it may add constraints in the form or type of
\par
innovations that are acceptable. It should be pointed out that regulation may actually help
\par
growth and innovation if consumer acceptance is seen as a problem and regulation is recognized
\par
as a solution for this problem (unfortunately, this is only theoretical; there is no evidence to date
\par
that regulation helps with consumer acceptance).xlvii
\par
E.7. The regulation creates barriers to entry or other domestic or international trade problems
\par
without explicitly analyzing those barriers. The regulation fails to analyze these problems.
\par
xlviii
\par
E.8. The regulation fails to consider the option of gathering more information to make a better-
\par
informed decision.xlix Options agencies should consider when there is a lot of uncertainty,
\par
20particularly as to the evidence of a problem or how to solve that problem include more research
\par
or pilot programs.
\par
E.9. The agency over interprets, selectively interprets, or misinterprets scientific research,
\par
including professional articles, surveys, focus groups, and experimental studies.l Agencies face
\par
conflicting pressures which may cause them to use science in an inappropriate way to support
\par
what they wish to do.
\par
E.10. The agency uses poorly researched (junk) science.li
\par
E.11. Assumptions are hidden or why particular assumptions were chosen is not explained.lii
\par
E.12. There is a loss of personal liberty that the agency has neither discussed nor analyzed.liii
\par
E.13. The agency claims benefits from a regulatory action but there are no costs associated with
\par
that action. All benefits and costs stem from changes in the market (changed behavior,
\par
technology, etc.) and stem from the requirement to do something different than is done now. As
\par
such, there are always opportunity costs that result from the mandated or voluntary change in
\par
behavior.liv
\par
E.14. The agency has failed to utilize one or more discount rates required by OIRA or has used a
\par
rate without sufficient explanation.lv
\par
E.15. The agency has failed to identify all of the parties affected by the rule.
\par
E.16. The agency has failed to identify the distribution of costs and benefits between different
\par
parties.
\par
21E.17. The agency has associated benefits and costs with legal requirements, as opposed to
\par
changed behavior. All benefits and costs result from changes in behavior, not legal
\par
requirements. This is a subtle point; legal requirements change how firms and consumers
\par
behave, although not always in the way intended. For example, the cost of a label requirement
\par
that puts a warning on food may be that the food is no longer made if the label is onerous enough
\par
to scare away consumers. The benefit would be the reduced risk from no longer consuming the
\par
food.lvi
\par
E.18. The agency has inappropriately counted transfers as costs or benefits. Transfers are a
\par
transfer of resources from one group to another, e.g., profit transfers that do not affect
\par
resources.14
\par
E.19. The agency has double counted benefits. consumption may improve I.Q. scores. to count both would be double counting benefits.
\par
For example, a regulation that reduces lead
\par
This in turn would lead to increases in productivity, but
\par
E.20. The agency claims that the regulation will protect consumers in some fashion but there is
\par
no data to support the claim. In many cases, what constitutes consumer benefits may be
\par
obvious. However, the presumption ought to be that the benefits for consumers will actually
\par
obtain by inferring the value consumers place from trades they make in the market (e.g., buying
\par
safer cars) or by directly eliciting values.
\par
lvii
\par
E.21. The agency has failed to note that an administrative threshold has been crossed---
\par
thresholds exist for the economic executive order (currently E.O. 12866), the Unfunded
\par
14 For regulatory flexibility analyses, economists will count estimate loss of profits as a cost for small businesses.
\par
However, for regulatory impact analyses, profits are simply transfers from consumers to producers; they do not
\par
constitute a loss of real resources (labor and capital).
\par
22Mandates Reform Act, and the Regulatory Flexibility Act (amended by the Small Business
\par
Reform and Enforcement Act).
\par
E.22. The agency analyzes only the costs and benefits of the activities required by the regulation,
\par
but not of the other changes that will come about because of the regulation (otherwise known as
\par
\texthorizontalbar indirect costs\textbardbl ). For example, the analysis of a regulation requiring more stringent checking of
\par
worker documentation that did not also consider the impact on the labor supply would be
\par
incomplete.lviii
\par
E.23. Distributional information on the risks and benefits are revealed while distributional
\par
information on the costs is concealed.
\par
E.24. Costs to Business
\par
In many cases, the regulatory options and costs of the rule will be the areas that businesses will
\par
know most about and are most likely to be the group that comments. It is important that
\par
businesses comment because they have the most direct knowledge of what their expenditures are
\par
likely to be.
\par
