\textbf{F. Risk}
\par
\emph{For regulations that address health or safety risks, agencies will often have prepared a risk}
\par
\emph{assessment. Although there are guidelines from OMB on how to do this, practices vary widely.}
\par
\emph{lix}
\par
\emph{Risk assessments may take the form of a safety assessment, which is a conservative finding of a}
\par
\emph{safe dose or emission, or they may take the form of an actual estimate of the risk the regulation}
\par
\emph{addresses.}
\par
F.1. The agency leaves out part of the science that argues against their case. In most risk
\par
assessments, particularly safety assessments that find a safe level or dose, studies that find no
\par
effect, even when rigorous, are thrown out and the results of only those studies that found
\par
positive results are averaged.lx
\par
27F.2. The agency fails to take into account risk/risk trade-offs.lxi Risk/risk trade-offs imply that
\par
certain actions may make society even worse off by raising risks that are not the target of the
\par
regulation.15 For example, this may be due to a change in what consumers purchase or a change
\par
in how a good is produced. The presence of risk/risk trade-offs is one reason that risk
\par
assessments should not be conservative. Of course, some risks that are not addressed by the
\par
target risk may also be reduced and strengthen the case for the regulation. A related type of
\par
consideration, health/health analysis is slightly more controversial. Health/health regulations
\par
theorize that by reducing private incomes to comply with regulations, less money will be spent
\par
on private risk reduction. Estimates range from around \$7.5 to \$15 million in regulatory
\par
expenditures per life lost.lxii
\par
F.3. The analysis makes conservative assumptions or utilizes conservative defaults that are not
\par
warranted. A conservative risk assessment makes assumptions that ensure that the risk is not
\par
underestimated or that the safety level ensures that absolutely no harm can be done. In both
\par
cases, it handcuffs risk managers by not informing them as to the true risks that they must weigh
\par
with other factors---such as costs or countervailing risks.
\par
F.4. The agency is addressing a problem that should be low priority compared to other problems
\par
in its purview.lxiii
\par
F.5. The analysis does not take into account baseline actions of market actors.
\par
\textbf{G. General Problems with Regulations}
\par
\emph{This section addresses general problems found in regulations.}
\par
15 An example of a risk/risk trade-off is not chlorinating water because of cancer concerns giving rise to problems
\par
with microbial pathogens.
\par
28G.1. The regulation is overly precautious to avoid under-regulation without being concerned
\par
about over-regulation. Precaution may make sense if there are large, irreversible effects and the
\par
science is tremendously uncertain. However, costs should be considered even in these cases.
\par
G.2 The regulation is vague about what it is trying to accomplish or exactly how it will
\par
accomplish it. Each regulation should have a goal or objective that is outcome oriented
\par
(something that people value like increased reading skills or reduced risk). There should also be
\par
a clear connection between the goal and an actual means of achieving that goal or objective.
\par
That means that the goal should be directly tied to compliance with the codified part of the
\par
regulation. These goals and means should be easy to understand and make logical sense. Even
\par
when a performance goal is established, it should address various ways that the goal can be
\par
accomplished.lxiv
\par
G.3. Logic. This is difficult to define but a commenter should check basic deductive and
\par
inductive rules of logic and look for logic fallacies that may present themselves. Clearly, there
\par
should not be emotional appeals in regulations or appeals to authority other than Congress.
\par
G.4. The regulation appears to ignore the findings from the regulatory impact analysis.lxv The
\par
regulatory impact analysis is supposed to inform Congress, the president, and stakeholders, but
\par
its primary use is to inform the executive branch decision maker.
\par
G.5. The agency violates its own guidance in how it will approach problems or how it will
\par
analyze problems (primarily risk assessments and benefit-cost analysis). Many agencies publish
\par
their own guidance on their Web sites.
\par
29G.6. The agency uses inappropriate baselines for comparison, e.g, comparing gains made in air
\par
quality to eastern Europe as though, without the regulations, U.S. air would become that bad.lxvi
\par
G.7. Unsubstantiated assertions are made in the regulation, i.e., statements made about consumer
\par
understanding or misunderstanding without supporting survey evidence. Often, agencies will
\par
cite anecdotes as evidence about a problem, practice, or level of knowledge and apply the
\par
information derived from the anecdotes to a population of firms or consumers inappropriately.
\par
G.9. The regulation will not solve the problem it intends to address. In some cases, the agency
\par
fails to make the case that even theoretically the regulation can work; in other cases, there is
\par
insufficient evidence that the remedy will work.
\par
G.10. The requirements in the codified section are vague and subject to arbitrary enforcement.
\par
The agency may claim that there are lower costs than would be the case if the rules were
\par
enforced on all members of an industry. This claim is made because the agency claims that
\par
provisions will be enforced at the discretion of the agency. Unless there is an accompanying
\par
explanation of exactly the conditions for enforcement, this is not a legitimate claim.lxvii
\par
G.11. Costs exceed benefits.lxviii There should be some explanation of why a regulatory option
\par
was chosen in which costs of complying with that option exceed the benefits of it.
\par
G.12. There is a better option than the one that is chosen.
\par
G.13. Agencies claim to rely on states for enforcement of the regulations without explicitly
\par
determining whether states are willing and able to enforce them.
\par
30G.14. Related to the previous comment. There is an unfunded mandate---a requirement that falls
\par
on states or localities without funding for them to carry it out.lxix Sometimes regulations are
\par
designed to minimize the burden on both the federal and state governments by transferring the
\par
burden to businesses.
\par
Effective Consumer Comments
\par
The issues raised above are ones that are essentially technical criticisms of regulations. They
\par
may very well persuade those of a technical bent that the regulation is not legal, has costs
\par
exceeding benefits, is not likely to accomplish its intended purpose, or will cause more harm
\par
(e.g., risk) than good. However, many policymakers are affected by the same kinds of non-
\par
technical considerations that affect ordinary consumers and these have been the subject of many
\par
years of psychological study.lxx The question for many policy makers for a particular regulation
\par
may be, \texthorizontalbar Is it the right thing to do?\textbardbl{} For example, if a risk regulation appears to protect a
\par
particularly vulnerable subpopulation, it is likely that the total cost may well be irrelevant for
\par
many people and policy makers.
\par
One additional way to comment on regulations is to address the acceptability of the approach to
\par
regulating risk. The concerns that drive people in risk situations are things like the degree of
\par
control they believe they have over the risk, whether there is the potential for catastrophe, the
\par
distribution of benefits and costs, and who is perceived to be at the cause of a problem.
\par
Addressing these kinds of issues, in addition to those technical concerns listed above, may
\par
provide decision makers with helpful information.
\par
31As mentioned earlier, comments on regulations are likely to be more effective if they are part of
\par
an organized group, even if only organized around a particular issue. Small businesses in an
\par
industry, for example, may be particularly more salient for an agency if they present a unified
\par
comment. This is not to say that a well-reasoned comment will not be effective, but regulatory
\par
agencies are political entities and, as such, politics will always play a role.
\par
Conclusion
\par
Regulations continue to affect a larger part of our lives and, once on the books, are rarely
\par
removed. While improvements have been made to this process since passage of the
\par
Administrative Procedures Act in 1946, there are still many people who are affected but are left
\par
out of the development process, some by choice, some because they do not use their democratic
\par
right to participate effectively. Because the regulations are difficult to understand and because
\par
many of the left-out do not have access to technical regulatory experts, more help in this area
\par
may lead to issue coalitions to address this inequality. This paper makes a small generic step
\par
toward helping those potential coalitions.
