\par
Abstract In this research we investigate how IT infra-
\par
structure capabilities are related to IT project success from
\par
a development team perspective. We first conduct an
\par
extensive literature review and summarize the insights to
\par
suggest an IT infrastructure base model. Drawing upon
\par
several other bodies of literature, particularly the psy-
\par
chology literature, we then build upon the base model to
\par
propose an integrative research model for IT project suc-
\par
cess that considers both actual and perceived effects of IT
\par
infrastructure capabilities. This research model argues that
\par
(1) teamwork quality mediates the effect of technical and
\par
human IT infrastructure capabilities on IT project success,
\par
and (2) team perceptions of both IT infrastructure and team
\par
capabilities shape team perceived likelihood of project
\par
success, which subsequently affects team commitment that
\par
is crucial to IT project success. We also propose a direct-
\par
effect model that directly links all constructs to IT project
\par
success so that we can test the efficacy of our proposed
\par
research model by comparing all three models. We then
\par
collect empirical data (n = 91) through an online survey of
\par
CIO/CTOs and team leaders. All three models are evalu-
\par
ated and compared using the partial least squares method.
\par
The results show strong support for the proposed research
\par
model except for two IT infrastructure components. We
\par
discuss the practical and theoretical implications of the
\par
findings, and suggest several ways this research can be
\par
extended.
