1 Introduction
\par
Information technology (IT) has long been considered a
\par
vital resource for organizations to create and sustain their
\par
competitive advantage [1--3]. IT investment and its impact
\par
on organizational performance have been studied by a
\par
number of researchers [4--6]. Successful IT projects act as
\par
enablers for business activities that improve products and
\par
enhance services. For example, Dow Corning Inc. gained
\par
the ability to provide global support and services to cus-
\par
tomers through the implementation of SAP R/3, an enter-
\par
prise system in 1998 [7]. In contrast, failed IT projects can
\par
cause substantial financial losses to an organization and
\par
even jeopardize its survival [8--10]. A widely cited example
\par
is FoxMeyer Drug Company, a \$5 billion company and the
\par
fourth largest pharmaceutical distributor in the U.S., which
\par
went bankrupt when it struggled unsuccessfully with the
\par
implementation of its enterprise resource planning system
\par
[11, 12].
\par
IT infrastructure is generally considered as the base of
\par
organizations' IT portfolio [13] and one of the most
\par
123124 important IT resources [14]. Consequently, IT infrastructure
\par
capabilities have become an indispensable factor in effec-
\par
tive organizational performance improvement. Researchers
\par
have examined how IT infrastructure capabilities affect
\par
organizational performance in terms of infrastructure ser-
\par
vices benefits [15], business transformation [13], business
\par
process redesign improvements [16, 17], international
\par
business performance [18, 19], and stock price [20].
\par
Organizational IT infrastructure capabilities affect organi-
\par
zational performance via information systems function and
\par
business processes [21]. Due to their vital roles, IT infra-
\par
structure capabilities, therefore, are a key construct in this
\par
study.
\par
Despite the abundance of research on IT infrastructure
\par
capabilities, the question of how organizations can leverage
\par
IT infrastructure capabilities to achieve IT project success
\par
remains unanswered. IT projects require a substantial
\par
amount of investment and they are critically related to
\par
organizational performance [22, 23]. For example, Alcoa
\par
has reduced operational cost by \$600 million in 2002
\par
through the adoption of Oracle as the standardized com-
\par
pany-wide database system [24]. An in-depth understand-
\par
ing of the relationship between IT infrastructure
\par
capabilities and IT project success certainly contributes to
\par
both IT project management theories and practices.
\par
Therefore, the goal of this study is to investigate this
\par
important relationship to bridge a major gap in the litera-
\par
ture. Specifically, this study addresses two research ques-
\par
tions: (1) What IT infrastructure capabilities are important
\par
to improving the quality of teamwork for IT project suc-
\par
cess? (2) How do team perceptions of both IT infrastruc-
\par
ture and team capabilities affect team commitment in
\par
achieving IT project success?
\par
Recognizing that IT projects are almost always devel-
\par
oped by teams, and team behavior and attitude have a large
\par
impact on project performance [25, 26], this study adopts a
\par
development team perspective to investigate how IT
\par
infrastructure capabilities are related to IT project success.
\par
We first summarize the current literature on the relation-
\par
ship between IT infrastructure capabilities and IT project
\par
success to derive Model 1, which we call the IT infra-
\par
structure base model. Then we adopt the development team
\par
perspective to propose Model 2, an integrative research
\par
model to examine both the actual and perceived effects of
\par
IT infrastructure capabilities. Specifically, Model 2 con-
\par
siders the role of teamwork quality and team commitment
\par
in this relationship: (1) teamwork quality mediates the
\par
effect of technical and human IT infrastructure capabilities
\par
on IT project success, and (2) team perceptions of both IT
\par
infrastructure and team capabilities shape team perceived
\par
likelihood of project success, which subsequently affects
\par
team commitment that is crucial to IT project success. We
\par
develop eight hypotheses and empirically test them using
\par
123
\par
Inf Technol Manag (2010) 11:123--142
\par
the partial least squares (PLS) technique. To fully gauge
\par
the meaningfulness and usefulness of Model 2, we not only
\par
compare it to Model 1, the base model but also Model 3, a
\par
direct-effect model that directly links all constructs to IT
\par
project success. The model comparison leads to a highly
\par
interesting finding: both actual and perceived effects of IT
\par
infrastructure capabilities are in fact equally important to a
\par
team in achieving IT project success. We also discuss the
\par
practical and theoretical implications.
\par
This paper is organized as follows. In Section 2, we first
\par
summarize the insights from the current literature on IT
\par
infrastructure capabilities as the IT infrastructure base
\par
model, and then we review other relevant literature to
\par
propose our integrative IT project success model and
\par
present research hypotheses with an emphasis on the model
\par
comparison approach. Section 3 describes the research
\par
methodology, and Section 4 presents the empirical results
\par
of the proposed integrative research model as well as
\par
model comparison results. Section 5 concludes the paper
\par
with a discussion on both theoretical contributions and
\par
practical implications. We also address the limitations of
\par
this study as well as suggestions on future research direc-
\par
tions for success in IT project management.
\par
2 Model development
\par
2.1 IT project success
\par
Organizations use IT projects as enablers to improve
\par
products and enhance services [7, 24], making IT project
\par
success an important and rewarding research topic. In this
\par
paper, the term IT project is used interchangeably with IS
\par
project, referring to information systems (IS) development
\par
to deploy business processes and services. In practice,
\par
successful completion of IT projects is highly challenging
\par
due to the inherent uncertainties associated with techno-
\par
logical complexities. According to the CHAOS report [27],
\par
only 34\% of the IT projects were successful in 2004.
\par
Studies also showed that the project management maturity
\par
is the lowest in the information systems application area
\par
[28]. On the other side, this low success rate presents huge
\par
improvement potential since IT projects usually require a
\par
substantial amount of investment and resources. The
\par
promising side of the challenge is that IT project success
\par
rate can be significantly improved through research. In fact,
\par
the 34\% IT project success rate, albeit still low, was
\par
already dramatically improved from 16\% reported a decade
\par
earlier [29], owning to research on system development life
\par
cycle, project office management, project risk manage-
\par
ment, and supplier management. Therefore, researchers
\par
need to continue their effort to explore new possibilities of
\par
achieving IT project success.Inf Technol Manag (2010) 11:123--142 IT project success has been studied extensively as a
\par
dependent variable for more than three decades. For
\par
example, early researchers have chosen time, cost, user
\par
satisfaction, and computer operations as IT project success
\par
criteria [30]. Robey and Farrow [31] developed a con-
\par
structive conflict model that used only conflict resolution as
\par
the dependent variable without any success criteria, but
\par
later their model was expanded to also include project
\par
success as the criterion variable [32]. IT project success is
\par
usually conceptualized as a multidimensional construct
\par
[33, 34] due to its complexity. Delone and McLean [35]
\par
proposed an updated IS success model and particularly
\par
discussed the application of the updated model in mea-
\par
suring e-commerce system success. Integrating theories of
\par
IS, organizational teams, and project management,
\par
Aladwani [36] found that support technologies including
\par
``production, coordination, and organizational dimensions''
\par
positively influence project performance.
\par
In addition to studying IT project success as a dependent
\par
variable, researchers have examined successful IT projects
\par
from various angles that form two main streams: (1) sys-
\par
tems development management and (2) IT project man-
\par
agement. Systems development management focuses on
\par
approaches and techniques for the development of IT
\par
projects that replace old business processes and synergis-
\par
tically reengineer them with technology. For example,
\par
organizations are often advised to apply the well-known
\par
system development life cycle (SDLC), which guides the
\par
partitioning of tasks into different phases to facilitate the
\par
development process for successful completion of IT pro-
\par
jects. Evaluating systems development techniques in terms
\par
of their usefulness and value for IT project success can lead
\par
to novel insights, for example, a situational approach for
\par
choosing appropriate system development tools and tech-
\par
niques contributes to IT project success [33].
\par
The other research stream, IT project management,
\par
focuses on project management practices that facilitate IT
\par
project development. For example, scope minimization,
\par
communications, and standard infrastructure are identified
\par
as three ingredients for the ``recipe for project success''
\par
[37]. One project management strategy to enhance IT
\par
project success is to establish a project management office
\par
[38]. Unfortunately, the value of project management
\par
office is not always beneficial and satisfactory, although it
\par
may enhance the IT infrastructure capabilities [39]. Suc-
\par
cessful IT project management often requires effective
\par
control of resources, risks, and budgets within the project
\par
[40]. Others have studied the impact of management
\par
commitment and control on IT project performance
\par
[41--43]. Important factors affecting project success include
\par
properly defined roles and responsibilities of an IT project
\par
team, teamwork, and motivation. These factors are equally
\par
or even more important to project success than factors such
\par
125
\par
as technical skills and training [44]. Project team skills can
\par
facilitate application and development skills, resulting in
\par
IT project success [45]. Project team's organizational cit-
\par
izenship behavior influences IT project success through
\par
integration climate and effective project management [46].
\par
In this study, we focus on IT infrastructure and its impact
\par
on IT project success.
\par
2.2 IT infrastructure
\par
IT infrastructure has been extensively studied and a rich
\par
body of literature is available (see Table 1 for a summary).
\par
Consistent with previous research, we define IT infra-
\par
structure as the foundation of the IT portfolio (including
\par
both technical and human assets), shared throughout the
\par
organization in the form of reliable services coordinated by
\par
an IT team [15, 47, 48]. IT infrastructure capabilities
\par
include both technical and managerial expertise required to
\par
provide reliable services. Therefore, IT infrastructure
\par
capabilities can be captured fully by dividing it into tech-
\par
nical IT infrastructure capability and human IT infra-
\par
structure capability [47, 49].
\par
Technical IT infrastructure capability is defined as
\par
functions provided by the technical IT infrastructure which
\par
is the set of choices pertaining to IT components and
\par
corresponding configurations such as application, data,
\par
system, and technology configurations [47]. According to
\par
the IT infrastructure analysis grid shown in Table 2 ([21],
\par
p. 29), technical IT infrastructure capability can be oper-
\par
ationalized into two dimensions: (1) functional efficiency
\par
of technical IT infrastructure components, and (2) flexi-
\par
bility of technical IT infrastructure components. Functional
\par
efficiency represents the extent to which technical IT
\par
infrastructure capability meets a project team's basic needs
\par
(ITREACH). A technical component is considered func-
\par
tionally efficient when its design goal is realized. Flexi-
\par
bility represents the extent to which technical IT
\par
infrastructure capability satisfies a project team's changing
\par
needs caused by IT and project changes (ITFLEX).
\par
Human IT infrastructure capability is defined as func-
\par
tionalities provided by the human IT infrastructure. These
\par
functionalities include choices pertaining to required
\par
knowledge and management skills of IT personnel to man-
\par
age IT resources within the organization effectively [47].
\par
Human IT infrastructure capability can also be operation-
\par
alized into two dimensions: (1) functional efficiency of
\par
human IT infrastructure components, and (2) flexibility of
\par
human IT infrastructure components, drawn from the IT
\par
infrastructure analysis grid [21]. Functional efficiency rep-
\par
resents the extent to which human IT infrastructure capa-
\par
bility provides the range of shared services (ITRANGE) and
\par
IS standards (ITSTAND) required by a project team. Flex-
\par
ibility represents the extent to which human IT infrastructure
