2.4 Teamwork quality
\par
The notion of ``team'' has become an important and nec-
\par
essary element for any organization to solve problems and
\par
improve productivity. According to the nature of team
\par
activities, teams can be classified into six types: (1) pro-
\par
duction team, (2) service team, (3) management team, (4)
\par
project team, (5) action team, and (6) parallel team [84].
\par
Our study focuses on project teams working on an IT
\par
project to develop, install, and implement computer sys-
\par
tems and applications in an organization.
\par
Teamwork quality refers to the quality of interactions
\par
within an IT project team and it has been identified as an
\par
antecedent to successful projects by Hoegl and Gemuenden
\par
[25]. They operationalized teamwork quality using six
\par
facets: communication, coordination, balance of member
\par
contributions, mutual support, effort, and cohesion. Their
\par
results suggest positive links from these six facets to team
\par
performance (i.e., effectiveness and efficiency) and per-
\par
sonal success (i.e., work satisfaction and learning). Team-
\par
work quality has also been adopted and validated in the IS
\par
context by examining the mediating effect of a group
\par
communication support system between teamwork quality
\par
and team performance [85]. The positive effect of team-
\par
work quality has been further studied in innovative projects
\par
from perspectives such as team autonomy [86], teams'
\par
domain-relevant and creative-thinking skills [87], and team
\par
member proximity [88]. Additionally, team member dis-
\par
persion was found to enhance the relationship between
\par
teamwork quality and team performance [89]. In summary,
\par
teamwork quality positively affects IT project success.
\par
Teamwork quality mediates the relationship between IT
\par
infrastructure capabilities and IT project success. Drawn
\par
from the resource-based theory, IT infrastructure has been
\par
recognized as a vital IT resource that contributes to busi-
\par
ness process improvement [14, 58]. Examples include the
\par
use of groupware technology to enhance team performance
\par
[90], and the use of intranet to facilitate teamwork [91]. In
\par
their well-known I/S success model [34], success is rec-
\par
ognized as ``a process construct which must include both
\par
temporal and causal influences in determining I/S success''
\par
(p.83). This body of literature suggests that IT infrastruc-
\par
ture, as a vital IT resource, contributes to team process
\par
interaction quality and in turn, positively affects IT project
\par
performance. In other words, teamwork quality plays an
\par
