2.5 Team attitude
\par
Having high-performing teams is fundamental to organi-
\par
zational success, including IT project success. Several IS
\par
project team studies have confirmed the positive effect of
\par
team participation, team members' perception and attitude,
\par
and personality on system success [92--94]. Attitude is
\par
defined as ``an internal state that influences an individual's
\par
choices of personal action, or a response tendency'' ([95],
\par
p.146) and it can influence the formation and development
\par
of high-performing teams.
\par
Team attitude in this study is comprised of four con-
\par
structs: perceived IT infrastructure capabilities, perceived
\par
team capabilities, perceived likelihood of project success,
\par
and team commitment based on prior literature [96]. The
\par
four constructs are illustrated in Fig. 2 and explained
\par
below.
\par
Perceived IT infrastructure capabilities is an IT project
\par
team's perception on whether IT infrastructure capabilities
\par
can facilitate projects effectively. Perceived teamInf Technol Manag (2010) 11:123--142 capabilities is an IT project team's perception of its ability,
\par
drawing from available IT infrastructure capabilities, to
\par
perform the project effectively. Perceived likelihood of
\par
project success is a project team's momentary belief con-
\par
cerning how likely the project will be successfully com-
\par
pleted. Team commitment is defined as an IT project team's
\par
determination and persistence put toward achieving the
\par
project goal.
\par
Expectancy theory explains the relationship between
\par
team attitude and team performance [73, 80]. Expectancy
\par
theory decomposes a person's motivation into valence and
\par
expectancy. Valence describes how much a person likes or
\par
dislikes doing something for a specific outcome and can be
\par
increased by raising rewards for a specific outcome. Mean-
\par
while, expectancy refers to a momentary belief that a specific
\par
behavior leads to a specific outcome. Expectancy can be
\par
increased by strengthening the connection between per-
\par
ceived capabilities to do something and the work outcome.
\par
Motivation plays a key role in achieving team perfor-
\par
mance. Keeping team members motivated is one of the
\par
eleven key characteristics of high-performing teams [97].
\par
Motivation management helps connect employee motiva-
\par
tion to performance and leverage the three keys (confi-
\par
dence, trust, and satisfaction) in a belief system model to
\par
build high-performing teams effectively and efficiently
\par
[98]. The factors that set high-performing teams apart from
\par
other ordinary teams ascribe to affective or psychological
\par
processes instead of work breakdown structures and pro-
\par
cedures [99].
\par
We propose that perceived IT infrastructure capabilities
\par
have a positive impact on perceived likelihood of project
\par
success. In other words, if an IT project team has positive
\par
perception of IT infrastructure capabilities, the team will
\par
have confidence to complete the project successfully. This
\par
proposition is built upon multiple streams of literature
\par
including the expectancy theory [73], the classic input-
\par
mediator-output model [100], the impact of team attitude
\par
on IT project success [72, 81], and the attitude to outcome
\par
studies addressing the effect of user perceived benefits on
\par
individual impact (e.g., [101, 102]). Following the same
\par
line of thinking, we also propose that perceived team
\par
capabilities positively affects perceived likelihood of pro-
\par
ject success, and in turn, affects team commitment to the
\par
successful completion of projects. For example, consider
\par
two IT project teams working on a project to develop an
\par
Oracle database application; team one has excellent Oracle
\par
development capabilities but team two does not have
\par
much. Consequently, team one has a higher perceived
\par
likelihood of project success than team two because these
\par
two teams have different perceptions of Oracle develop-
\par
ment capabilities. As a result, team one has a higher team
\par
commitment than team two due to their difference in per-
\par
ceived likelihood of project success. Summarizing the
\par
129
\par
arguments above, we propose the following three
\par
hypotheses:
\par
Hypothesis H4 (Perceived ITI Capabilities and Perceived
\par
Likelihood of Project Success Hypothesis) Perception of
\par
IT infrastructure capabilities positively shapes an IT pro-
\par
ject team's perception of likelihood of project success.
\par
Hypothesis H5 (Perceived Team Capabilities and Per-
\par
ceived Likelihood of Project Success Hypothesis) Per-
\par
ception of team capabilities positively shapes an IT project
\par
team's perception of likelihood of project success.
\par
Hypothesis H6 (Perceived Likelihood of Project Success
\par
and Team Commitment Hypothesis) Perceived likelihood
\par
of project success positively affects team commitment.
\par
Studies about both direct and indirect effects of goal
\par
commitment on task performance reveal that goal com-
\par
mitment is a critical factor for studying task performance
\par
from the task recipient's attitude perspective [103, 104].
\par
Expectancy theory has been used to explain the goal
\par
commitment process [105]; researchers not only examined
\par
the moderator effect of goal commitment between goal
\par
level and task performance but also proposed a positive
\par
relationship between expectancy of goal attainment and
\par
goal commitment. The importance of goal commitment has
\par
been examined in prior research and results in general
\par
suggest that it strongly influences project success. For
\par
instance, based on two simulated IS project experiments, it
\par
was found that ``commitment to an information systems
\par
(IS) development project is essential for systems success''
\par
[72]. Another study found that individual, group, and
\par
institutional commitments influence a sales and marketing
\par
IT project development process [106]. Team commitment
\par
has been well recognized as one of the most important IT
\par
project success factors [82, 83, 100, 107]. Another study
\par
used a function of performance, behavior, attitude, team
\par
member style, and corporate culture to predict team
\par
effectiveness in a mathematical model [108]. Team com-
\par
mitment not only affects IT project success but also con-
\par
nects to teamwork quality. An IT project team with a
\par
strong perceived likelihood of project success often has a
\par
strong determination to complete the project. Specifically,
\par
high team commitment motivates members of an IT project
\par
team to work hard, resulting in high teamwork quality.
\par
Consequently, the team can complete the project more
\par
effectively, efficiently, and satisfyingly. In short, team
\par
commitment leads to teamwork quality and project per-
\par
formance. The above discussion leads to the following two
\par
hypotheses:
\par
Hypothesis H7 (Team Commitment and Teamwork
\par
Quality Hypothesis) Team commitment positively affects
\par
teamwork quality
\par
123130 Hypothesis H8 (Team Commitment and IT Project Suc-
\par
cess Hypothesis) Team commitment positively affects IT
\par
project success.
\par
3 Research methodology
\par
We collected empirical data from project teams to test and
\par
compare the proposed research model to the other two
\par
alternative models. Specifically, we conducted an online
\par
survey through SurveyMonkey.com which minimizes the
\par
data entry errors in traditional paper-based surveys and
\par
provides a less costly data collection method.
\par
3.1 Survey respondents
\par
We randomly selected 680 organizations from Lead411
\par
Inc. We also added 20 organizations from one of the
\par
author's social networks. All 700 organizations are U.S.
\par
companies. We then sent survey invitations via email to the
\par
CIO/CTOs of these organizations. We invited them to
\par
complete the first online questionnaire and forward the
\par
second questionnaire link to the team leader of the most
\par
recently completed IT project within their organization. If
\par
the CIO/CTO happened to be the team leader of the most
\par
recently completed IT project, the individual was invited to
\par
complete the second questionnaire as well.
\par
3.2 Measurement items
\par
We used existing scales from the literature whenever pos-
\par
sible. Validity of existing scales has been proven in past
\par
studies, hence lending high reliability to our results. We also
\par
developed several new measurement items for new con-
\par
structs, following protocols well-established in the literature.
\par
IT project success can be measured by the extent to
\par
which an IT project team completes the project effectively,
\par
efficiently, and satisfyingly. An instrument (ITPERM) has
\par
been developed and empirically validated by earlier studies
\par
[32, 36]. We used that instrument and adapted its items.
\par
We adapted the instrument developed by Xia [21] for the
\par
measurement of both technical and human IT infrastructure
\par
capability. As discussed earlier, technical IT infrastructure
\par
capability has two dimensions. The functional efficiency
\par
dimension was measured by the reach of IT infrastructure
\par
(ITREACH), and the flexibility dimension was measured
\par
by ITFLEX. For human IT infrastructure capability, the
\par
functional efficiency dimension was measured by items of
\par
range of shared IT infrastructure services (ITRANGE) and
\par
IS standards and procedures (ITSTAND), and the flexi-
\par
bility dimension was measured by IS management com-
\par
petence (ITMGT). We adapted items to measure teamwork
\par
quality (TWQ) from prior research [25].
\par
123
\par
Inf Technol Manag (2010) 11:123--142
\par
We developed a new instrument for perceived IT
\par
infrastructure capabilities and perceived team capabilities
\par
because we could not identify an existing one from the
\par
literature. Our development effort closely followed those
\par
reported in the literature [109, 110]. Perceived IT infra-
\par
structure capabilities (PITIC) were assessed by asking the
\par
project team leaders about their perceived usefulness,
\par
availability, and support of IT infrastructure resources.
\par
Perceived team capabilities (PTC) were assessed by asking
\par
the project team leaders about their confidence of cooper-
\par
ation, attitude, and skills/knowledge to complete the pro-
\par
ject. Perceived likelihood of project success (PLPS) was
\par
assessed by the project team leaders retrospectively. PLPS
\par
is essentially the same as the IT project success instrument
\par
ITPERM except we used future tense in the questions. This
\par
ensures consistency and simplifies the measurement effort.
\par
To measure team commitment (COMMIT), we adapted the
\par
validated scale of goal commitment [111, 112]. All existing
\par
and new scales and their sources are summarized in Table 3.
\par
Before using the survey in the large scale data collec-
\par
tion, we pretested the survey instrument by a focus group
\par
composed of business faculty, IT staff, and graduate stu-
\par
dents. Based upon their suggestions, we shortened and
\par
reworded some items to improve clarity and eliminate
\par
ambiguity. The finalized measurement items are shown in
\par
the ``Appendix: Questionnaire items''. A 7-point Likert
\par
scale ranging from 1 (strongly disagree) to 7 (strongly
\par
agree) was used to measure all items in ITPERM, ITFLEX,
\par
ITSTAND, ITMGT, TWQ, PITIC, PTC and PLPS. The
\par
COMMIT items were measured using a 7-point Likert
\par
scale ranging from 1 (very low) to 7 (very high). The
\par
ITREACH items were measured using an 11-point scale
\par
ranging from 0 to 100 percentages. The ITRANGE items
\par
were measured using 3-point scale ranging from 1 (cen-
\par
tralized), 2 (decentralized), and 3 (outsourced). Measurement
