3.3 Sample characteristics
\par
We deleted 95 CIO/CTOs from the sample because their
\par
e-mail addresses were either invalid or undeliverable. The
\par
effective number of invitations was hence 605. We
\par
received 106 responses to the first questionnaire and 95
\par
responses to the second one. Two and four responses were
\par
unusable for the first and second questionnaires respec-
\par
tively, and were removed from the sample. We then mat-
\par
ched 91 responses from both questionnaires. The final
\par
response rate is 15.04\%. This is a decent response rate that
\par
compares favorably to other similar ``matched pair'' IT
\par
infrastructure surveys (e.g., [113]). Most respondents to the
\par
first questionnaire were CIO/CTOs (86.54\%). A variety of
\par
industries were represented in the analysis with the com-
\par
puter/software (26.92\%) and banking/finance/insurance
\par
(19.23\%) industries well represented. Other industries
\par
include manufacturing (16.35\%), restaurant/hotel (10.58\%),
\par
agriculture (5.77\%), wholesale/retail (4.81\%), healthcare
\par
(4.81\%), education (4.81\%), and others.
\par
Nearly 29\% of the respondent firms had the number of
\par
full-time IT employees between 50 and 99. The number of
\par
full-time IT employees in the range of 25--49 and 100--999
\par
accounted for about 20\% of the respondents. The majority
\par
of IT projects were developed by small teams. Specifically,
\par
about 80\% of the IT projects were developed by teams with
\par
fewer than 19 members. Nearly 97\% of the projects were
\par
completed within 12 months. Most IT projects were com-
\par
pleted within 4 to 6 months. Only 2.20\% were completed
\par
within 13--35 months, and no projects were completed in
\par
more than 3 years.
\par
We followed the convention to assess the non-response
\par
bias by comparing the information retrieved from early
\par
respondents and late respondents [114]. Using the midpoint
\par
of the data collection period as the cutoff point [115],
\par
59.43\% of the first questionnaire respondents were classi-
\par
fied as early respondents and the rest as late respondents.
\par
We compared the two groups by their job titles and the
\par
number of full-time IT employees. Similarly, 60\% of the
\par
second questionnaire respondents were classified as early
\par
respondents and the rest as late respondents. We compared
\par
them based on the number of team members in the IT
\par
project and the timeframe within which the IT project
\par
completed. We conducted Chi-square tests to find no sig-
\par
nificant differences between early and late responses for
\par
both questionnaires. Therefore, we concluded that non-
\par
response bias is not a serious threat to the results of this
\par
study.
\par
4 Data analysis
\par
4.1 Measurement instrument validation
\par
The first step of the analysis is the assessment of mea-
\par
surement instrument validity. Since the scales used are
\par
either existing ones or developed based on the literature,
\par
content validity of the measurement instrument is well
\par
established. We then factor analyzed the constructs to
\par
assess the convergent validity. The analysis examined
\par
whether all measurement items for a specific construct
\par
loaded onto that construct as expected. Generally, factor
\par
loading serves as the assessment criterion. An item needs to
\par
have a factor loading [0.4 to be considered acceptable
\par
[116]. Preferably, the factor loading needs to be [ 0.6 for
\par
the item to be considered convergent [117]. Therefore, we
\par
eliminated items with loadings \textbackslash 0.4 or cross loaded onto
\par
two or more factors with loadings [0.6.
\par
We also assessed the reliability of constructs using
\par
Cronbach's alpha. Nunnally [118] suggested alpha of 0.7
\par
for existing scales and 0.6 for newly developed scales. In
\par
addition, if an item's factor loading and corrected item-
\par
total correlation are low, and Cronbach's alpha increases
\par
dramatically after deleting this item, then the item is
\par
eliminated.
\par
ITREACH has five items. All factor loadings are [0.8
\par
and all items exhibit high corrected item-total correlations
\par
ranging from 0.5210 to 0.7698. Cronbach's alpha for the
\par
construct ITREACH is 0.8612. Therefore, all five items are
\par
retained. For ITFLEX, the fourth item (ITFLEX4) does not
\par
load onto the expected factor with the other items. The
\par
corrected item-total correlation of ITFLEX4 is-0.1998.
\par
Deleting ITFLEX4 leads to a significant improvement of
\par
Cronbach's alpha from 0.6304 to 0.8204. Therefore,
\par
ITFLEX4 was eliminated from the construct. ITRANGE
\par
has eight items. It is a shortened and adapted version of the
\par
21-item instrument developed by Xia [21]. All items have
\par
high factor loadings and no serious cross loading issues are
\par
identified. Cronbach's alpha for the construct ITRANGE is
\par
0.7550. For ITSTAND, all items have high factor loadings
\par
and corrected item-total correlations. Cronbach's alpha for
\par
the construct ITSTAND is 0.8815. The construct ITMGT
\par
has seven items. The fifth item (ITMGT5) does not load
\par
onto the expected factor with the rest of items. The
\par
corrected item-total correlation of ITMGT5 is-0.1921.
\par
Deleting the item leads to an increase of Cronbach's alpha
\par
from 0.75 to 0.8633. ITMGT5 therefore is eliminated from
\par
the construct.
\par
All eight TWQ items load onto a single factor and
\par
Cronbach's alpha for the construct is 0.9151. Perceived IT
\par
infrastructure capabilities and perceived team capabilities
\par
both have three items. They all load as expected with
\par
loadings [0.7. Cronbach's alpha is 0.8939 and 0.8449,
\par
123132 respectively. All seven PLPS items load onto a single
\par
factor with loadings [0.7 and corrected item-total corre-
\par
lations ranging from 0.6392 to 0.7667. Cronbach's alpha
\par
for PLPS is 0.8992. For the COMMIT construct, five items
\par
load onto one single factor as expected and corrected item-
\par
total correlations of the five items range from 0.68 to
\par
0.8689. The COMMIT construct has a Cronbach's alpha of
\par
0.9144. The construct ITPERM has seven items and high
\par
corrected item-total correlations are observed for all items
\par
except ITPERM5. However, removing ITPERM5 does not
\par
significantly increase Cronbach's alpha of ITPERM. It only
\par
increases from 0.8092 to 0.8124. Therefore, ITPERM5 is
\par
retained in the scale. Table 4 presents Cronbach's alpha for
\par
all constructs.
\par
4.2 Partial least squares (PLS)
\par
Partial least squares (PLS) is an advanced statistical
\par
method that ``facilitates testing of the psychometric prop-
\par
erties of the scales used to measure a variable, as well as
\par
estimating the parameters of a structural model---that is,
\par
the magnitude and direction of the relationships among the
\par
model variables'' [110, 119]. PLS is an effective multi-
\par
variate technique that has been widely applied in MIS
\par
research [120--123]. Compared to the conventional
\par
covariance-based technique, PLS has the advantage of
\par
being less demanding on data. PLS can work with small
\par
data sets with many missing values. But with a large
\par
sample size, PLS can leverage the statistical power to reach
\par
strong conclusions [124].
\par
We made sure that the sample size is sufficiently large to
\par
warrant the appropriate use of PLS to assess the mea-
\par
surement model [125]. The software package used in this
\par
study is PLS-Graph 3.0 [126]. The minimum sample size
\par
required for PLS is usually determined by the larger one of
\par
(1) ten times the measurement scale with the largest
\par
number of formative indicators, or (2) ten times the largest
\par
number of independent latent variable impacting a depen-
\par
dent latent variable [127]. Since no formative indicators are
\par
used in this study, the minimum sample size required is 60
\par
(ten times six independent latent variables). Our sample
\par
size of 91 is more than adequate.
\par
4.3 Measurement model assessment
\par
In addition to measurement instrument validation via factor
\par
analysis, we used PLS-Graph 3.0 to assess the measure-
\par
ment model. We examined discriminant validity of con-
\par
structs used in the model and reliability of the measurement
\par
model. We looked at average variance extracted (AVE),
\par
individual item weights and loadings, and composite reli-
\par
ability index in the assessment.
