\par
133
\par
the square root of AVE is greater than correlations between
\par
the construct and any other constructs [127, 129]. This
\par
criterion ``may be overly restrictive in some contexts''
\par
([130], p.151). In this study, 52 of the total 55 off-diagonal
\par
correlations are lower than their diagonal elements, and the
\par
three are only slightly higher. This indicates strong support
\par
for discriminant validity of the constructs. Finally, all off-
\par
diagonal correlations in Table 5 are \textbackslash 0.9, indicating that
\par
the constructs are distinct [131].
\par
We then assessed the reliability of each item by its
\par
weight and loading. The individual item weight is the
\par
regression beta coefficient of the item on its latent construct
\par
and can be interpreted as each item's contribution to its
\par
latent construct [127]. The individual item loading repre-
\par
sents the correlation between each item and its latent
\par
construct. Chin [127] also suggested that item loadings
\par
should be[0.707 and any item with a loading\textbackslash 0.5 should
\par
be dropped. Items with loadings between 0.5 and 0.6 are
\par
acceptable when the current context differs from the ori-
\par
ginal context in which the instrument was developed [132].
\par
Reliability of each latent construct was assessed by com-
\par
posite reliability (CR) index. CR is a way to examine the
\par
internal consistency of items for the same latent construct
\par
[127] and should be higher than 0.7 [129].
\par
We found that most items have a loading [ 0.707. Only
\par
a few items have a loading between 0.5 and 0.7, which
\par
is completely acceptable. We removed ITRANGE1 and
\par
ITRANGE8 because their loadings are \textbackslash 0.4. The elimi-
\par
nation of these two items leads to an improvement of CR of
\par
ITRANGE. It increased from 0.806 to 0.836. CR for all
\par
latent constructs range from 0.836 to 0.936, indicating
\par
strong internal consistency of constructs in the measure-
\par
ment model.
\par
4.4 Structural model assessment and model
\par
comparison results
\par
We first evaluated all three models using PLS. In structural
\par
model assessment using PLS, two measures are particularly
\par
important: the squared multiple correlations (R2) and path
\par
coefficients [133]. Chin [127] argued that R2 for each
\par
endogenous construct in the PLS model can be interpreted
\par
identically as in a regression model: it measures the per-
\par
centage of an endogenous construct's variation explained
\par
by the model. Thus, a high R2 provides strong support to
\par
the usefulness of the model. Path coefficients represent
\par
``the paths among the constructs/measures'' [110]. Sizeable
\par
path coefficients hence are interpreted as strong relations
\par
between constructs. The standard error estimation for
\par
parameters is obtained through bootstrapping [126]. We
\par
performed the bootstrapping procedure with a resampling
\par
of 200 in PLS-Graph 3.0 and then used the estimated
\par
standard error to test the significance of path coefficients.
