The estimation results for all three models are reported in
\par
Figs. 3, 4 and 5.
\par
The PLS results for all three models are then compared
\par
to evaluate the meaningfulness and usefulness of the inte-
\par
grative research model. A model comparison approach is
\par
highly desired in research using PLS because models
\par
technically are not ``confirmed'' [134]. PLS evaluates a
\par
theoretical model's predictability and a good result is
\par
interpreted as a strong support to the model [116, 134].
\par
However, the possibility of other alternative plausible
\par
models cannot be ruled out. Methodologists thus have
\par
suggested that model comparison is a more appropriate use
\par
of the technique [116, 129]. Through comparison, the
\par
strength and weakness of a model is sufficiently exposed,
\par
hence researchers can be more confident about research
\par
conclusions.
\par
Model 1 is the IT infrastructure base model that is
\par
inferred from the existing literature. The model examines
\par
whether a significant correlation exists between IT infra-
\par
structure capabilities and IT project success. The result
\par
shows that all IT infrastructure components except
\par
ITREACH are significantly related to IT project success.
\par
This is not a surprising result given the abundant sup-
\par
porting literature. Moreover, the R2 of Model 1 is a highly
\par
respectable 60.6\%, indicating that IT infrastructure capa-
\par
bilities can explain a large portion of the variation in IT
\par
project success. This significant result provides strong
\par
support to the proposed mediating relationship in Model 2:
\par
a significant correlation between the independent and
\par
dependent variables is the premise for a mediation model to
\par
stand [135].
\par
Model 2 is the proposed integrative research model
\par
regarding how IT infrastructure capabilities affect IT pro-
\par
ject success. The model considers both the actual and
\par
perceived effects of IT infrastructure capabilities. Team-
\par
work quality is proposed to mediate the actual effect and
\par
team commitment is the mediator for the perceived effect.
\par
The result shows that the model is a significant one with
\par
large R2 values for all four endogenous constructs. The
\par
overall model R2 is 57.8\%, a very high value among similar
\par
peer studies. All path coefficients are significant except two
\par
IT infrastructure components. In short, the proposed inte-
\par
grative research model received strong support from the
\par
empirical data.
\par
To assess the meaningfulness and usefulness of the
\par
proposed structural paths in Model 2, we also evaluated the
\par
direct-effect model (Model 3) that skips all the structural
\par
relations in Model 2. The overall R2 of Model 3 (66.9\%) is
\par
higher than that of Model 2 (57.8\%), but this difference is
\par
indeed minimal and it should not be interpreted as Model 3
\par
is better. This is because the evaluation of model-data fit in
\par
PLS must be based on a comprehensive examination of all
\par
R2 values. Model 2 has four endogenous constructs:
\par
teamwork quality (TWQ), perceived likelihood of project
\par
success (PLPS), team commitment (COMMIT), and IT
\par
project success (ITPERM). All four R2 have large values
\par
and are highly significant in the F test (Table 6). Clearly,
\par
this demonstrates a nice model-data fit. On the other side,
\par
some parameter estimates in Model 3 are particularly dis-
\par
turbing: the path coefficient for perceived IT infrastructure
\par
capabilities (PITIC) and perceived team capabilities (PTC)
\par
are insignificant, and the path coefficient for perceived
\par
likelihood of project success (PLPS) is even a negative
\par
value, all of which are apparently not in line with the
\par
literature. This type of result usually appears when
\par
researchers do not sufficiently consider the mediating
\par
relationship between variables. Since PLPS mediates the
\par
relationship between PITIC, PTC and team commitment
