123Inf Technol Manag (2010) 11:123--142 5 Discussion and conclusion
\par
In this study we proposed an integrative research model
\par
(Model 2) that relates IT infrastructure capabilities to IT
\par
project success. Adopting a development team perspective,
\par
the proposed model considered both the actual and perceived
\par
effects of IT infrastructure capabilities and further argued
\par
that the effect is mediated by teamwork quality and team
\par
commitment, respectively. We then collected empirical data
\par
to test the proposed model. We found strong support for
\par
Model 2, particularly in comparison to two alternative
\par
models: an IT infrastructure base model (Model 1) inferred
\par
from the current literature, and a direct-effect model
\par
(Model 3) that skips all the internal structural relations in the
\par
proposed model. Most hypotheses in Model 2 received
\par
strong empirical support. This study found that teamwork
\par
quality positively mediates the actual effect of IT infra-
\par
structure capabilities on IT project success, while team
\par
commitment positively mediates the perceived effect of IT
\par
infrastructure capabilities on IT project success. Among all
\par
findings, the most interesting and insightful one is that both
\par
actual and perceived effects of IT infrastructure capabilities
\par
are in fact equally important to a team in achieving IT project
\par
success.
\par
5.1 Theoretical contributions
\par
This study contributes to the literature in several ways. To
\par
the best of our knowledge, this is the first study that
\par
directly addresses the relationship between IT infrastruc-
\par
ture capabilities and IT project success. Both are important
\par
research topics in the IS/IT literature, but the relationship
\par
between them was not theoretically examined and empir-
\par
ically tested, although it can be inferred from the resource-
\par
based theory that IT infrastructure capabilities have a
\par
positive impact on IT project success. This study empiri-
\par
cally validated the positive effect of IT infrastructure
\par
capabilities on IT project success, hence bridging an
\par
important gap in the literature. Further, insights from this
\par
study also suggest researchers to consider how organiza-
\par
tions can leverage IT infrastructure capabilities to improve
\par
IT project success rate, in addition to traditional project
\par
management and development methodology management
\par
approaches.
\par
A more important theoretical contribution is the con-
\par
sideration of both actual and perceived effects of IT
\par
infrastructure capabilities. In this study, we adopted a
\par
development team perspective to consider both team
\par
behavior and team attitude. Through model comparison,
\par
this study found that both actual and perceived effects of IT
\par
infrastructure capabilities are important to the success of IT
\par
projects, and the effect is mediated by teamwork quality
\par
and team commitment, respectively. In the development of
\par
137
\par
the proposed model, we heavily consulted the psychology
\par
literature. Specifically, we relied on expectancy theory and
\par
the classic input-mediator-output model to link team
\par
members' perception to their project performance via team
\par
commitment. Our results validate both theories in the
\par
context of IT project teams. More importantly, our results
\par
contribute to further theory development about IT infra-
\par
structure capabilities and IT project success. In short, the
\par
incorporation of theories from other disciplines can be a
\par
fruitful approach to IS/IT research.
\par
The use of IT project success as the dependent variable
\par
is potentially a promising research approach. In this study
\par
we focused on examining the effect of IT infrastructure
\par
capabilities on IT project success instead of organizational
\par
performance. The impact of IT infrastructure capabilities
\par
on organizational performance is certainly an important
\par
issue but it is also a difficult issue because the impact is
\par
difficult to quantify due to its long-term and intangible
\par
nature. In contrast, studying their impact on IT project
\par
success actually provides a new means to quantify the
\par
organizational benefits of IT infrastructure capabilities: an
\par
organization hosts a portfolio of projects so the success of
\par
the organization can be measured by the success of pro-
\par
jects. We expect our study to inspire more interest in this
\par
research stream.
\par
5.2 Practical implications
\par
The results of our study contribute to IT project manage-
\par
ment practice. Our study clearly shows that IT infrastruc-
\par
ture capabilities of an organization are highly important
\par
and relevant to IT project success. Organizations hence
\par
should always consider building up stronger IT infra-
\par
structure capabilities for better IT project success rate.
\par
Specifically, our study suggests that two IT infrastructure
\par
components seem to be much more critical than others: a
\par
development team with flexible technical IT infrastructure
\par
components and mature IT standards. They lead to high
\par
teamwork quality that in turn leads to IT project success.
\par
While organizations should not ignore other IT infra-
\par
structure components, these two components are critical
\par
and deserve significant managerial attention.
\par
Our study informs IS/IT managers that perception of
\par
capabilities is also important. While the actual effect of IT
\par
infrastructure capabilities is significant, the perception of
\par
IT infrastructure capabilities and team capabilities also
\par
play a critical role. Highly confident teams produce good
\par
results. It hence is a practical approach for IS/IT managers
\par
and team leaders to leverage IT infrastructure capabilities
\par
to improve a development team's confidence in project
\par
success. With high confidence, teams are more committed,
\par
which in turn leads to high teamwork quality and IT project
\par
success.
\par
123138 5.3 Limitations of the study
\par
Like any other study, this study is subject to limitations
\par
resulting from the tradeoffs that researchers often make
\par
to operationalize a study within feasibilities in time and
\par
costs. One possible limitation is the common source bias.
\par
While we aimed to have different respondents for the
\par
two different surveys, some CIO/CTOs completed both
\par
surveys because they were also serving as team leaders
\par
for the most recently completed project. We conducted a
\par
one-way ANOVA to compare the mean and variance of
\par
the two subgroups, one with single respondents and one
\par
with multiple respondents. The results were not signifi-
\par
cant though. Common source bias, thus, is not likely to
\par
present a major threat to our findings. Second, using
\par
perceived measures is a common limitation to this type
\par
of studies. Perceptual data collected from survey
\par
respondents will always be subject to individual experi-
\par
ences, retrospective memories, and personal judgment.
\par
Respondents may choose to respond in a favorable
\par
manner for their organization or team. Although we tried
\par
to mitigate this risk by implementing a strong protocol
\par
maintaining anonymity and confidentiality, we acknowl-
\par
edge the limitation associated with our study with respect
\par
to this risk. Finally, we requested respondents to identify
\par
the most recently completed IT projects to avoid the
\par
memory contamination effect. This also made responding
\par
to our survey easier. However, this method cannot thor-
\par
oughly measure different types of IT projects developed
\par
in different industries. The fact that the survey data came
\par
from multiple industries largely alleviated the concern.
\par
Despite our efforts to minimize the effect that these
\par
limitations can have on the insights that the results pro-
\par
vide, the reader should still be cautious about general-
\par
izing the findings of this study beyond the scope of the
\par
settings defined in this study.
\par
Our study can be extended in multiple ways in future
\par
work. The proposed model may need slight refinement
\par
since not all the hypotheses are supported. As mentioned
\par
above, we only collected data on the most recently com-
\par
pleted IT projects. Defining IT projects from different
\par
perspectives such as development methodology, project
\par
size, and project completion time can lead to promising
\par
future research opportunities. Doing so can increase the
\par
generalizability of the results. Specifically, researchers can
\par
investigate the moderating effect of IT project type on the
\par
relationship between IT infrastructure capabilities and IT
\par
project success. Replication of the current study with a
\par
larger sample is also a promising direction. With a large
\par
sample size, it is possible to test the proposed research
\par
model using the covariance-based approach. The covari-
\par
ance-based approach can provide rich statistical measures
\par
123
\par
Inf Technol Manag (2010) 11:123--142
\par
such as model-data fit, which provides more insights about
\par
how models can be refined.
