\par
Submodularity and Partitioning
\par
Wenhui Liao, Qiang Ji, \emph{Senior Member, IEEE}, and William A. Wallace, \emph{Fellow, IEEE}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Abstract}---As sensors become more complex and prevalent, they}
\par
\textbf{present their own issues of cost effectiveness and timeliness. It}
\par
\textbf{becomes increasingly important to select sensor sets that provide}
\par
\textbf{the most information at the least cost and in the most timely and}
\par
\textbf{efficient manner. Two typical sensor selection problems appear}
\par
\textbf{in a wide range of applications. The first type involves selecting}
\par
\textbf{a sensor set that provides the maximum information gain within}
\par
\textbf{a budget limit. The other type involves selecting a sensor set}
\par
\textbf{that optimizes the tradeoff between information gain and cost.}
\par
\textbf{Unfortunately, both require extensive computations due to the}
\par
\textbf{exponential search space of sensor subsets. This paper proposes}
\par
\textbf{efficient sensor selection algorithms for solving both of these}
\par
\textbf{sensor selection problems. The relationships between the sensors}
\par
\textbf{and the hypotheses that the sensors aim to assess are modeled}
\par
\textbf{with Bayesian networks, and the information gain (benefit) of}
\par
\textbf{the sensors with respect to the hypotheses is evaluated by mutual}
\par
\textbf{information. We first prove that mutual information is a submod-}
\par
\textbf{ular function in a relaxed condition, which provides theoretical}
\par
\textbf{support for the proposed algorithms. For the budget-limit case,}
\par
\textbf{we introduce a greedy algorithm that has a constant factor of}
\par
(1- 1/e) \textbf{guarantee to the optimal performance. A partitioning}
\par
\textbf{procedure is proposed to improve the computational efficiency}
\par
\textbf{of the algorithms by efficiently computing mutual information}
\par
\textbf{as well as reducing the search space. For the optimal-tradeoff}
\par
\textbf{case, a submodular--supermodular procedure is exploited in the}
\par
\textbf{proposed algorithm to choose the sensor set that achieves the}
\par
\textbf{optimal tradeoff between the benefit and cost in a polynomial-time}
\par
\textbf{complexity.}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Index Terms}---Active fusion, Bayesian networks (BNs), sensor}
\par
\textbf{selection, submodular function.}
