I. INTRODUCTION
\par
\textbf{M}ANY real-world applications use sensors to obtain in-
\par
formation that will help them improve their activities.
\par
Here, sensor is a general term; it could refer to a test, a
\par
feature, an observation, an evidence, etc. Rarely is only one
\par
sensor needed, however. Most applications use several sensors
\par
of different kinds, which we refer to as sensor sets. As sensors
\par
become more prevalent and ubiquitous, they present their own
\par
issues of cost effectiveness and timeliness. It becomes critically
