\par
important to select sensor sets that provide the most information
\par
at the least cost in a timely and efficient manner. For example, in
\par
fault diagnosis problems (medical diagnosis, computer system
\par
troubleshooting, etc.) [8], [49], a set of informative tests needs
\par
to be selected to provide an optimal tradeoff between the cost
\par
of performing the tests and the accuracy of diagnoses. In sensor
\par
networks [11], [26], [27], a subset of sensors needs to be
\par
selected to achieve a suboptimal tradeoff between the energy
\par
consumption of operating the sensors and the information gain.
\par
In pattern recognition [21], [33], [42], good features need to be
\par
selected to guarantee the performance of the classifiers.
\par
Purposely choosing an optimal subset from multiple sens-
\par
ing sources can save computational time and physical costs,
\par
avoid unnecessary or unproductive sensor actions, reduce re-
\par
dundancy, and increase the chance of making correct and timely
\par
decisions. Because of these benefits, sensor selection plays
\par
a particularly important role for time-critical and resource-
\par
limited applications, including computer vision, control sys-
\par
tems, sensor networks, diagnosis systems, and many military
\par
applications.
\par
Basically, sensor selection problems can be divided into
\par
two types. The first type, called a budget-limit case, involves
\par
choosing a sensor set with maximum information gain given a
\par
budget limit. The other type, called an optimal tradeoff case,
\par
involves deciding a sensor set that achieves an optimal tradeoff
\par
between the information gain and the cost. Unfortunately, both
\par
of them are NP-hard, since the number of sensor subsets grows
\par
exponentially with the total number of sensors.
\par
Most work formulates sensor selection as an optimization
\par
problem based on information-theory or decision-theoretic cri-
\par
teria. However, solving the optimization problem efficiently
\par
is difficult since the search space usually is large. To be
\par
practical, some methods [11], [26], [45] select the best sensor
\par
myopically or select the first m sensors greedily. However,
\par
the selected sensors could lead to poor performance, since the
\par
selection methods cannot provide performance guarantees. Re-
\par
cently, some sensor selection algorithms have been proposed to
\par
achieve a balance between performance and efficiency. Isler and
\par
Bajcsy [18] present an approximation algorithm for sensor se-
\par
lection that aims to minimize the error in estimating the position
\par
of a target within a sensor network. They prove that at least one
\par
of the sensor subsets whose sizes are less or equal to six can
\par
guarantee that the resulting estimation error is within a factor of
\par
two of the least possible error under certain assumptions. Thus,
\par
the algorithm is to enumerate all l-subsets (l {$\leq$} 6) of sensors
\par
and choose the best one. Zheng \emph{et al. }[49] use a greedy test-
\par
selection strategy to find an optimal subset of tests in a fault
\par
diagnosis system. Similarly, this paper provides a theoretical
