\par
We seek to achieve a balance between the performance and
\par
the efficiency of the proposed sensor selection algorithms by
\par
fully utilizing the properties of submodular functions and the
\par
probabilistic relationships among sensors. The probabilistic re-
\par
lationships between the sensors and the hypotheses are modeled
\par
by a Bayesian network (BN). In addition, the informativeness
\par
of the sensors with respect to the hypotheses is measured by
\par
mutual information. We then prove that mutual information is
\par
a submodular function under several conditions. Based on the
\par
theory of submodular functions, in the budget-limit case, the
\par
proposed sensor selection algorithms provide a constant factor
\par
of (1- 1/e) guarantee to the optimal performance. Further-
\par
more, we propose a partitioning procedure by exploiting sensor
\par
dependence embedded in the BN model to compute mutual
\par
information efficiently as well as to reduce the search space, so
\par
that the efficiency of the algorithms is further improved. In the
\par
optimal tradeoff case, a submodular--supermodular procedure
\par
is embedded within the proposed sensor selection algorithm to
\par
decide the optimal or near-optimal sensor set that maximizes
\par
the difference between benefit and cost with polynomial-time
\par
complexity.
\par
