suggestions for future work.
\par
II. RELATED WORK
\par
Sensor selection usually consists of two problems: selec-
\par
tion criterion definition and sensor set selection based on the
\par
defined sensor selection criterion. In general, sensor selection
\par
can be treated as an optimization problem: finding an optimal
\par
sensor set that maximizes the objective function defined by
\par
the selection criterion. Most existing work can be divided into
\par
two groups based on the sensor selection criterion they used:
\par
information-theoretic methods and decision-theoretic methods.
\par
\emph{A. Sensor Selection Criteria}
\par
Information-theoretic methods apply information theory to
\par
define the objective function for sensor selection. The common
\par
functions include Shannon's entropy, mutual information, en-
\par
783
\par
tropy difference, and Kullback--Leibler's (KL) cross-entropy.
\par
Hintz [17] uses the expected change in Shannon entropy when
\par
tracking a single target moving in one dimension with Kalman
\par
filters. Zhang \emph{et al. }[48] apply mutual information to select
\par
an optimal sensor set based on a dynamic BN. Denzler and
\par
Brown [9] use mutual information to determine the optimal ac-
\par
tion (set of camera parameters, including focal length, pan, and
\par
tilt angles) that will maximally decrease the uncertainty of the
\par
object-state estimation process. Guo and Nixon [16] use mu-
\par
tual information to select feature subsets for gait recognition.
\par
Wang \emph{et al. }[45] propose an entropy-based sensor selection
\par
heuristic for target localization. Instead of using mutual infor-
\par
mation, they use maximal entropy difference as the criterion to
\par
choose one sensor at each step until the required uncertainty
\par
level of the target state is achieved. The method is computa-
\par
tionally simpler than the mutual-information-based approaches,
\par
and their experiments demonstrate that the method can sort
\par
candidate sensors into exactly the same order as the mutual-
\par
information method does in most cases.
\par
An active sensing approach, based on an entropy measure
\par
called the R\'enyi divergence, is proposed by Kreucher \emph{et al. }[28]
\par
to schedule sensors for multiple-target tracking applications. At
\par
each time step, only one sensor action is chosen to provide
\par
measurement and thus update the probability density of the
\par
target states. KL's cross-entropy is used for optimal multisensor
\par
allocation [35] and sensor management [24]. Ertin \emph{et al. }[11]
\par
employ expected posterior entropy to choose the next mea-
\par
surement node (sensor) in a distributed Bayesian sequential
\par
estimation framework. They show that minimizing the expected
\par
posterior uncertainty is equivalent to maximizing the mutual
\par
information between the sensor output and the target state.
\par
Although information-based criteria (entropy, mutual infor-
\par
mation, etc.) are the most commonly used functions for ranking
\par
information sources in terms of uncertainty reduction, these cri-
\par
teria require intensive computations, particularly when comput-
\par
ing for multiple sensors. Therefore, the research reported in the
\par
aforementioned papers often selects only one sensor at one time
\par
with the information-theoretic criteria. We propose an efficient
\par
procedure in this paper to efficiently compute mutual informa-
\par
tion by exploiting the statistical independences among sensors.
\par
For decision-theoretic approaches, the objective function
\par
is defined based on decision theory, where the goal is to
\par
choose the optimal sensory action by maximizing an over-
\par
all utility function (e.g., tradeoff between cost and benefit).
\par
Wu and Cameron [47] describe a mathematical framework
\par
using Bayesian decision theory to select optimal sensing actions
\par
for achieving a given goal, for example, for object recognition
\par
in robot vision applications where one optimal action is de-
\par
cided at each time step. Rimey and Brown [41], [40] build a
\par
task-oriented computer vision system that uses BN and a
\par
maximum-expected-utility decision rule to choose a sequence
\par
of task-dependent and opportunistic visual operations on the
\par
basis of their utilities.
\par
van der Gaag and Wessels [44] build a decision tree for
\par
selective gathering of evidence for diagnostic belief networks.
\par
Lindner \emph{et al. }[32] estimate the expected utility of each sensor
\par
to predict the minimum cost subset of sensors for a mobile robot
\par
application. Kristensen [29] develops a Bayesian decision tree
\par
Authorized licensed use limited to: IEEE Transactions on SMC Associate Editors. Downloaded on November 22, 2009 at 09:48 from IEEE Xplore. Restrictions apply.784 IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SYSTEMS, MAN, AND CYBERNETICS---PART A: SYSTEMS AND HUMANS, VOL. 39, NO. 4, JULY 2009
\par
to solve the problem of choosing proper sensing actions from a
\par
family of candidates. Each sensing action is evaluated by its ex-
\par
pected interest from sample information (EISI). The Bayesian
\par
decision rule simply selects the one with the maximum EISI
\par
value as the sensing action to be performed one at a time.
\par
The most common decision-theoretic criterion is the value of
\par
information (VOI). The VOI of a sensor set is defined as the
\par
difference in the maximum expected utilities with and without
\par
the information collected from the sensor set. It evaluates the
\par
utility of the sensor set by considering both the benefit and cost
\par
of using the sensors. The cost of operating the sensor for evi-
\par
dence collection includes the computational cost, physical cost,
\par
etc., while the benefits include financial benefits, performance
\par
improvements, reduced loss, etc. Oliver and Horvitz [37], [38]
\par
apply an expected-value-of-information (EVI)-based policy to
\par
selective perception in SEER, a multimodal system of recog-
\par
nizing office activity that relies on a layered hidden Markov
\par
model (LHMM) representation. Although the system uses a
\par
dynamic programming strategy to compute the EVI of each
\par
feature combination based on the LHMM, it needs to enumerate
\par
all the feature combinations and then selects the best one. The
\par
process is therefore time-consuming.
\par
A special group in the decision-theoretic methods is based on
\par
Markov decision process (MDP). Bayer-Zubek [2] formalizes
\par
the diagnosis process as an MDP to find an optimal diagnostic
\par
policy that achieves optimal tradeoffs between the costs of tests
\par
and the costs of misdiagnoses. Cassandra \emph{et al. }[3] model the
\par
problem of deciding optimal actions for mobile-robot naviga-
\par
tion as a POMDP. Castanon [4] formulates the problem of dy-
\par
namic scheduling of multimode sensor resources for classifying
\par
a large number of stationary objects as a POMDP.
\par
The decision-theoretic methods provide a precise formula-
\par
tion for sensor selection problems. These measures are usu-
\par
ally different from the information-theoretic measures such as
\par
mutual information since they directly relate sensor/resource
\par
allocation to decision making, while information theoretic
\par
criterion relates sensor management to situation/event assess-
\par
ment. On the other hand, like the information-theoretic criteria,
\par
decision-theoretic criteria also require significant computation
\par
for multiple sensors. As a result, like the information-theoretic
\par
criteria, most of the aforementioned methods adopt the myopic
\par
approach, i.e., choose only one sensing action at each step. For
\par
example, MDP-based approaches suffer from combinatorial
\par
explosion when solving practical problems of even moderate
\par
size. Another problem of the decision-theoretic methods is
\par
the subjective definition of utility functions. The utilities are
\par
usually problem dependent and may vary as their environment
\par
changes. In addition, for some applications, it may not be
\par
possible to derive appropriate utilities.
\par
Heuristic searches use some function that estimates the cost
\par
from the current state to the goal, presuming that such a
\par
function is efficient. Heuristic search techniques incorporate
\par
domain knowledge to improve efficiency over blind search.
\par
Some heuristic searches can guarantee an optimal solution, but
\par
they could be very slow. Thus, another search strategy is to give
\par
up completeness and risk losing optimal subsets in exchange for
\par
efficiency, such as \emph{sequential forward selection }[46], \emph{floating}
\par
\emph{search selection }[39], and simulated annealing.
\par
However, due to the high computational cost of search al-
\par
gorithms, in most applications involved with sensor selection,
\par
the greedy strategy is used. This strategy can be regarded
\par
as the simplest form of sequential search, where, at each iter-
\par
ation, the best sensor is incorporated into the candidate sensor
\par
set until there is no improvement in the value of the objective
\par
function. A more complex sequential search approach, called
\par
entropy adaptative aggregation algorithm, is proposed in [12]. It
\par
includes an aggregative phase to heuristically choose the initial
\par
subset and an adaptative phase to iteratively aggregate and dis-
\par
aggregate the current subset until it converges. Kalandros \emph{et al.}
\par
[23] explore the use of randomization and superheuristics
\par
search [31] for sensor selections in target-tracking applications.
\par
The search begins with a base sensor set and then generates
\par
more alternative solutions via a probabilistic assignment rule.
\par
The best solution is decided only from the generated solutions,
\par
and so, it may not be optimal. Kundakcioglu and Unluyurt [30]
\par
integrate concepts from one-step look-ahead heuristic algo-
\par
rithms and basic idea of Huffman coding to construct a
\par
minimum-cost AND/OR decision tree bottom-up for sequential
\par
fault diagnosis. In [1], Amari and Pham develop a method to
\par
provide lower and upper bounds on the optimal number of
\par
spares for each subsystem of complex repairable systems. In
\par
this way, the search space is reduced dramatically and a near-
\par
optimal solution can be found efficiently.
\par
Our work differs from the foregoing work in that it explicitly
\par
exploits the theory of submodular functions with respect to the
\par
sensor selection criterion, and the probabilistic relationships
\par
among sensors to achieve both efficient and accurate sensor
\par
selections in two typical scenarios: the budget-limit case and
\par
the optimal-tradeoff case. Hence, the proposed algorithms are
\par
not only efficient but also they provide performance guarantees.
\par
\emph{B. Optimization Methods for Sensor Selection}
\par
For both the information-theoretic and decision-theoretic
\par
criteria, optimization methods are needed to identify the opti-
\par
mal sensor set. Methods for solving the optimization include
\par
search methods and mathematical programming (e.g., approxi-
\par
mate dynamic programming, integer programming, and greedy
\par
selection strategies). One option is to use heuristic searches.
\par
III. SENSOR SELECTION AND FUSION MODEL
\par
We use dynamic BNs, as shown in Fig. 1, to model the
\par
relationships between sensors and the hypotheses the sensors
\par
aim to assess. Given the dynamic Bayesian network (DBN),
\par
sensor fusion is performed through probabilistic inference. A
\par
BN is a directed acyclic graph that represents a joint proba-
\par
bility distribution among a set of variables [5], [22]. In a BN,
\par
nodes denote variables and the links between nodes denote the
\par
conditional dependences among the variables. The dependence
\par
for each node is characterized by a conditional probability
\par
table. A DBN additionally models the temporal relationships
\par
of the variables. Such a model is capable of representing the
\par
relationships among different sensors in a coherent and unified
\par
hierarchical structure, accounting for sensor uncertainties and
\par
dependences, modeling the dynamics in situation development
