IV. INFORMATION GAIN AND SENSOR COST
\par
The goal of sensor selection is to select a group of sensors
\par
that achieves a good balance between the information gain
\par
(benefit) and the cost of the sensors. In recent years, a com-
\par
monly used method to measure information gain is mutual
\par
information. With respect to Fig. 1(a), let S be a sensor set, S=
\par
\textbraceleft S1,S2,...,Sn\textbraceright; the mutual information of S with respect to
\par
{$\Theta$}, I({$\Theta$};S), is defined as follows: I({$\Theta$};S) = H({$\Theta$})- H({$\Theta\vert$}S),
\par
where H indicates the entropy function. I({$\Theta$};S) measures the
\par
uncertainty reduction of {$\Theta$} given the sensors in S.
\par
Mutual information may have a very interesting property,
\par
called \emph{submodularity}. Before we prove it, we first give the
\par
background information about submodular functions.
\par
Let V be a finite set, and let f be a set function: 2V {$\rightarrow$} {$\mathfrak{R}$}. A
\par
function f is \emph{submodular }if the inequality
\par
f(A) + f(B) {$\geq$} f(A {$\cap$} B) + f(A {$\cup$} B)
\par
holds for every pair of sets A, B {$\subseteq$} V [34]. Equivalently
\par
f(A {$\cup$} X)- f(A) {$\geq$} f(B {$\cup$} X)- f(B)
\par
for {$\forall$}A {$\subset$} B {$\subset$} V , X {$\in$} V . It means that the marginal value of
\par
X with respect to A is larger than the marginal value of X with
\par
respect to a larger set such as B. In other words, for submodular
\par
functions, adding X into a smaller set helps more than adding
\par
it into a larger set. For example, entropy functions, cut capacity
\par
functions, and matroid rank functions are submodular.
\par
The negative of a submodular function is called a \emph{supermod-}
\par
\emph{ular }function. In other words, a function f is supermodular if
\par
the inequality
\par
f(A) + f(B) {$\leq$} f(A {$\cap$} B) + f(A {$\cup$} B)
\par
holds for every pair of sets A, B {$\subseteq$} V . In addition, a function
\par
that is both submodular and supermodular is called a \emph{modular}
\par
function.
\par
In general, mutual information is not a submodular function
\par
[27]. However, we show that it is submodular under several
\par
conditions.
\par
\emph{Proposition 1: }Let A be any subset on S and f(A) =
\par
I({$\Theta$};A). If any sensor in S is conditionally independent of each
\par
other given {$\Theta$}, then f is submodular.
\par
\emph{Proof: }See Appendix.
\par
Although people usually make the assumption of conditional
\par
independence in their frameworks such as in [27] and [49], it is
\par
still a strong assumption for most applications. Proposition 2
\par
shows that f is still a submodular function under a relaxed
\par
assumption.
\par
\emph{Proposition 2: }Let A, B be any subset on S and f(A) =
\par
I({$\Theta$};A). If I({$\Theta$};B \textbackslash{} A\textbar A {$\cap$} B) {$\geq$} I({$\Theta$};B \textbackslash{} A\textbar A) or I({$\Theta$};A \textbackslash{}
\par
B\textbar A {$\cap$} B) {$\geq$} I({$\Theta$};A \textbackslash{} B\textbar B), then f is a submodular function.
\par
\emph{Proof: }See Appendix.
\par
I({$\Theta$};B \textbackslash{} A\textbar A {$\cap$} B) {$\geq$} I({$\Theta$};B \textbackslash{} A\textbar A) shows that the mutual
\par
information between {$\Theta$} and the sensors in B \textbackslash{} A is related
\par
to how much information is already known. It is reasonable
