VIII. ILLUSTRATIVE APPLICATION
\par
We apply the proposed sensor selection algorithms to an
\par
application of multistage battlefield situation assessment. The
\par
scenario here is inspired from the study in [7]. In this applica-
\par
tion, dynamic BN is used to model the sensors and hypothesis.
\par
Although we focus on static BNs in the previous sections, our
\par
algorithms can also apply to dynamic BNs one frame at a time,
\par
as shown in this application.
\par
The scenario develops during a period of growing hostility
\par
between the Blue force and the Red force who poses a threat.
\par
The goal of this scenario is for the Blue force to selectively use
\par
its surveillance assets to quickly and efficiently infer the intent
\par
of the Red force. More detail is given in [7]. The Blue force
\par
surveillance facilities include a number of offshore sensors,
\par
unmanned aerial vehicles, surveillance helicopters (RAH66
\par
Comanche), etc. The Blue forces on duty in the restricted zone
\par
consist of the following: 1) a Fremantle Class Patrol Boat
\par
(FCPB); 2) a Maritime Patrol Aircraft (MPA); 3) a Night Hawk
\par
Helicopter; and 4) one F111 (Maritime Strike Aircraft). The
\par
Red forces include the following: 1) a major fleet unit; a Guided
\par
Missile Frigate (FFG); 2) one FCPB; and 3) a communication
\par
ship. In addition, the Red force has two surface units armed
\par
with an M386 Rocket Launcher and a 110 SF Rocket Launcher
\par
that are ready to move to the locations where the Blue force is
\par
within their fire range.
\par
A dynamic BN, as shown in Fig. 5, is constructed to assess
\par
the situations for the scenario above. A set of hypotheses
\par
representing possible Red force intents includes the following:
\par
1) Passive---monitor the Blue forces in the restricted zone and
\par
assume that the Blue forces will not interfere with the fuel sup-
\par
ply; 2) Defensive---conduct active reconnaissance and maintain
\par
a defensive presence to guard the supply routes against the
\par
Blue force interference; and 3) Offensive---mount a naval attack
\par
or infantry artillery engagement (surface-to-air or surface-to-
\par
surface attack) on the Blue forces with the intent of destroying
\par
the Blue forces as well as their offshore surveillance facilities.
