\par
scenarios: the budget-limit case in which the best sensor set is
\par
the one with maximum information gain under a budget limit,
\par
and the optimal-tradeoff case in which the best sensor set is the
\par
one that achieves the optimal tradeoff between the information
\par
gain and the cost. Although finding an optimal solution is NP-
\par
hard for both of them, we introduce efficient and near-optimal
\par
solutions by fully utilizing the properties of the sensor selection
\par
criterion and the probabilistic dependences among sensors.
\par
Specifically, for the budget-limit case, to ensure performance
\par
of the proposed algorithms, we first prove that mutual informa-
\par
tion is a submodular function under a relaxed condition. Based
\par
on this property, we introduce an efficient greedy approach with
\par
a constant factor of (1- 1/e) performance guarantee to the
\par
optimal performance. Furthermore, to improve the efficiency
\par
of the algorithms, we propose a partitioning procedure for
\par
both efficient sensor selection and efficient mutual information
\par
computation. For the optimal tradeoff case, if the cost function
\par
is a modular function, the proposed algorithm can provide
\par
the global optimal solution in polynomial time; if the cost
\par
function is a submodular function, a submodular--supermodular
\par
procedure is embedded with the proposed sensor selection
\par
algorithm to choose the optimal or near-optimal sensor set in
\par
polynomial time. The experimental results with both synthetic
\par
and real data demonstrate the performance and efficiency of our
\par
algorithms.
\par
This paper focuses only on sensor selection. Our future goal
\par
is to model sensor selection, sensor fusion, and decision making
\par
in a unified framework. More issues will be addressed, e.g., how
\par
to fuse the information collected from the sensors efficiently,
\par
how to decide the optimal action based on the fused results, and
\par
how to learn the parameters of the BN framework. In addition,
\par
we will apply the framework as well as the algorithms to more
\par
real-world applications.
