\textbf{Active and Dynamic Information Fusion for Multisensor}
\par
\textbf{Systems With Dynamic Bayesian Networks}
\par
Yongmian Zhang and Qiang Ji
\par
\textbf{\emph{Abstract---}Many information fusion applications are often characterized}
\par
\textbf{by a high degree of complexity because: 1) data are often acquired from}
\par
\textbf{sensors of different modalities and with different degrees of uncertainty; 2)}
\par
\textbf{decisions must be made efficiently; and 3) the world situation evolves over}
\par
\textbf{time. To address these issues, we propose an information fusion framework}
\par
\textbf{based on dynamic Bayesian networks to provide active, dynamic, purposive}
\par
\textbf{and sufficing information fusion in order to arrive at a reliable conclusion}
\par
\textbf{with reasonable time and limited resources. The proposed framework is}
\par
\textbf{suited to applications where the decision must be made efficiently from dy-}
\par
\textbf{namically available information of diverse and disparate sources.}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Index Terms---}Active sensing, Bayesian networks, information fusion.}
\par
I. INTRODUCTION
\par
There has been a great deal of interest in the development of sys-
\par
tems capable of using many different sources of sensory information
\par
[1], [2]. Whatever the application may be, there is a need to systemati-
\par
cally and efficiently interpret the large volume of information acquired
\par
from sensors of different modalities and with different degrees of un-
\par
certainty. Typically, many applications contain a large number of un-
\par
certain events interrelated by causes and effects. The question is how to
\par
systematically and efficiently represent and fuse uncertain information
\par
at different levels of abstraction.
\par
The world situation is often dynamic and uncertain in nature and un-
\par
folds over time. To correctly assess and interpret the dynamic environ-
\par
ment, a fusion system is needed that not only can systematically handle
\par
uncertain sensory data of different modalities but, more importantly,
\par
can reason over time. The inability of current sensor fusion systems to
\par
correlate and reason about a vast amount of information over time is an
\par
impediment to providing a coherent overview of the unfolding events.
\par
The question is, therefore, how to account for the temporal changes in
\par
sensory information.
\par
Moreover, many applications are often constrained by limited
\par
time and resources. The usage of more sensors incurs more cost
\par
in acquiring information. It is important to avoid unnecessary or
\par
unproductive sensor actions and computations. Thus, we must select a
\par
subset of sensors that are the most decision-relevant. Now the question
\par
is how to determine a set of most informative information sources for
\par
the current goal with minimal cost at particular stage of information
\par
gathering to achieve an efficient and timely decision.
\par
To address above issues, a fusion system therefore requires the capa-
\par
bility which can not only represent the temporal changes of uncertain
\par
sensory information, but dynamically select the most relevant sensory
\par
data for a given goal at a given time as well. To achieve this, we propose
\par
to cast information fusion into a framework of dynamic Bayesian net-
\par
works (DBNs) to account for the temporal aspect of decision making
\par
and uncertainty of knowledge, and to integrate and infer dynamic sen-
\par
sory information of different modalities. The fusion system is able to
\par
actively select a subset of sensors to produce the most decision-rele-
\par
vant information with limited resources and in reasonable time. Addi-
\par
tionally, DBNs enable sensor selection to dynamically adapt to varying
\par
situations.
\par
Research in information fusion is getting wider and deeper nowa-
\par
days. There are a number of methods available for sensor fusion in-
\par
cluding evidential reasoning [3], fuzzy theories [4]--[6], and neural net-
\par
works [7], [8]. However, these methods lack the sufficient expressive
\par
power to handle uncertainties, dependencies and dynamics exhibited
\par
by sensory data in many applications. Bayesian networks (BNs), since
\par
their inception, have shown great promise in performing multisensor
\par
data fusion [1]. Recently, DBNs extend BNs for modeling dynamic
\par
events [9]--[13]. In the existing works, BNs and DBNs are primarily
\par
used for knowledge and uncertainty representation. DBNs were pro-
\par
posed as a generalization of hidden Markov models (HMMs) [14], but
\par
they allow much more general graph structures than an HMM does. It is
\par
therefore natural to consider a DBN as a basis of the general spatio-tem-
\par
poral sensor data analysis and interpretation.
\par
Active sensing involves actively controlling sensors to optimize in-
\par
formation gathering in a knowledge-based manner with an identifiable
\par
selection criterion rather than randomly selecting sensor parameters. A
\par
significant amount of research has been directed to the area of com-
\par
puter vision and robotics [15]--[19]. More recently, Oliver \emph{et al. }[20]
\par
studied selective perception policies to purposively guide sensing and
\par
visual information processing to circumvent the computational burden
\par
associated with perceptual analysis. Pinz \emph{et al. }[21] is the first to intro-
\par
duce active fusion in remote sensing image understanding. Paletta and
\par
Pinz proposed an active recognition system [22], where mutual infor-
\par
mation is used to quantify the ambiguity and to determine which view
\par
to select. Denzler and Brown [23] applied mutual information theory
\par
in the state estimation process for active camera parameter selection.
\par
Using HMMs and evidential reasoning to combine instantaneous and
\par
temporal visual information is recently studied in [24]. The notion of
\par
dynamically combining information provides useful hint to this work.
\par
A central problem of active information fusion is the sensor selection
\par
problem. There have been several attempts in sensor selection for target
\par
localization [25]--[28]. In theses works, they assumed that the system
\par
model is either a linear system or a standard stochastic model. Other
\par
works of interest for sensor selection can be found in the control liter-
\par
ature. The work presented in [29] considers an Hidden Markov model
\par
with a number of sensors. The sensor sequence is determined via sto-
\par
chastic dynamic programming. The problem of optimal sensor selec-
\par
tion for discrete-event systems under partial observation was studied
\par
in [30]. Since we use a DBN as an information fusion method, the
\par
above approaches of sensor selection can not directly be applied to our
\par
problem.
\par
This paper aims to formalize a framework based on DBNs for ac-
\par
tive and dynamic information fusion, which is particularly suited to
\par
the applications where the decision must be made efficiently from dy-
\par
namically available information of diverse and disparate sources. The
\par
remainder of this paper is organized as follows. We start with infor-
\par
mation fusion using DBNs in the next section. An active and dynamic
\par
information fusion framework is proposed in Section III. Section IV
\par
presents an example for a proof-of-concept. The final section is the
\par
conclusion.
