V. CONCLUSIONS
\par
There are three important issues for a fusion system: 1) modeling un-
\par
certainties of sensory data; 2) modeling temporal change; and 3) active
\par
control and management of the fusion process. In this paper, we pro-
\par
posed a framework to simultaneously address the above three issues.
\par
Toward the first and the second issue, we adopt a dynamic Bayesian
\par
network as a fusion structure to provide a coherent and fully unified hi-
\par
erarchical probabilistic framework for representation, integration, and
\par
inference of uncertain sensory information of different modalities at
\par
different levels of abstraction, and to account for the temporal aspect
\par
of decision making. Toward the last issue, we need an efficient sensor
\par
selection method that allows a fusion system to actively select and in-
\par
voke a subset of sensors that is the most decision-relevant.
\par
It is impractical to simply implement information-theoretic criterion
\par
in (7) to identify an optimal sensor subset because (7) generally requires
\par
time exponential in the number of summations to compute mutual in-
\par
formation exactly. In this paper, we mainly focus on the architectural
\par
concept and issues for active and dynamic information fusion rather
\par
than the efficient sensor selection methodology behind this framework.
\par
In many fusion applications, the relationships among the events are
\par
mostly unknown and they may vary as the world situation evolves.
\par
Therefore, a fusion system needs to incorporate a situation oriented
\par
learning mechanism. This is another issue that we will focus on in our
\par
future work.
