\par
sampling [7]. Other methods are proposed to overcome the dis-
\par
advantages of EM and Gibbs sampling. For example, methods are
\par
proposed to learn the parameters when data are not missing at ran-
\par
dom, such as the AI\&M procedure [8], the RBE algorithm [9], and
\par
the maximum entropy method [10,11]; some methods are proposed
\par
to escape local maxima under the assumption of MAR, such as the
\par
information-bottleneck EM (IB-EM) algorithm [12], data perturba-
\par
tion method [13], etc.; other methods are proposed to speed up the
\par
learning procedure, such as generalized conjugate gradient algorithm
\par
[14], online updating rules [15], and others.
\par
When data are missing completely at random, in other words,
\par
when several hidden nodes exist, those methods could fail, where
\par
the learned parameters may be quite different from the true param-
\par
eters. In fact, since there are no data for hidden nodes, learning pa-
\par
rameters becomes an ill-posed problem. Thus, prior data on domain
\par
knowledge are needed to regularize the learning problem. In most
\par
domains, at least some information, either from literature or from
\par
domain experts, is available about the model to be constructed. How-
\par
ever, many forms of prior knowledge that an expert might have are
\par
difficult to be directly used by existing machine learning algorithms.
\par
Therefore, it is important to formalize the knowledge systematically
\par
and incorporate it into the learning. Such domain knowledge can
\par
help regularize the otherwise ill-posed learning problem, reduce the
\par
search space significantly, and help escape local maxima.
\par
This motivates us to propose a Bayesian network learning al-
\par
gorithm for the case when multiple hidden nodes exist by sys-
\par
tematically combining domain knowledge during learning. Instead
\par
of using quantitative domain knowledge, which is often hard to
\par
obtain, we propose to exploit qualitative domain knowledge. Qual-
\par
itative domain knowledge impose approximated constraints on
\par
some parameters or on the relationships among some parameters.
\par
These kind of qualitative knowledge are often readily available.
\par
Specifically, two qualitative constraints are considered, the range
\par
of parameters, and the relative relationships between different pa-
\par
rameters. Instead of using the likelihood function as the objective
\par
to maximize during learning, we define the objective function as a
\par
combination of the likelihood function and the penalty functions
\par
constructed from the domain knowledge. Then, a gradient-descent
\par
procedure is systematically integrated with the Expectation-step (E-
\par
step) and Maximization-step (M-step) of the EM algorithm, to esti-
\par
mate the parameters iteratively until it converges. The experiments
\par
show the proposed algorithm significantly improves the accuracy
\par
of the learned BN parameters over the conventional EM method.
\par
2. Related work
\par
During the past several years, many methods have been pro-
\par
posed to learn BN parameters when data are missing. Two standard
\par
learning algorithms are Gibbs sampling [7] and EM [6]. Gibbs sam-
\par
pling by Geman and Geman [7] is the basic tool of simulation and
\par
can be applied to virtually any graphical model whether the arcs are
\par
directed or not, and whether the variables are continuous or discrete
\par
[16]. It completes the samples by inferring the missing data from the
\par
available information and then learns from the completed database
\par
(imputation strategy). Unfortunately, Gibbs sampling method suf-
\par
fers from convergence problems arising from correlations between
\par
successive samples [10]. In addition, it is not effective when data
\par
are missing in complete random (e.g. the case of the hidden
\par
nodes).
\par
The EM algorithm can be regarded as a deterministic version of
\par
Gibbs sampling used to search for the Maximum Likelihood (ML) or
\par
Maximum a Posteriori (MAP) estimate for model parameters [16,6].
\par
However, when there are multiple hidden variables or a large amount
\par
of missing data, EM gets easily trapped in a local maximum. ``With
\par
3047
\par
data missing massively and systematically, the likelihood function
\par
has a number of local maxima and straight maximum likelihood
\par
gives results with unsuitably extreme probabilities'' [17]. In addition,
\par
EM algorithms are sensitive to the initial starting points. If the initial
\par
starting points are far away from the optimal solution, the learned
\par
parameters are not reliable.
\par
Different methods are proposed to help avoid local maxima.
\par
Elidan and Friedman [12] propose an information-bottleneck EM
\par
(IB-EM) algorithm to learn the parameters of BNs with hidden
\par
nodes. It treats the learning problem as a tradeoff between two
\par
information-theoretic objectives, where the first one is to make the
\par
hidden nodes uninformative about the identity of specific instances,
\par
and the second one is to make the hidden variables informative
\par
about the observed attributes. However, although IB-EM has a better
\par
performance than the standard EM for some simple BNs, it is actu-
\par
ally worse than EM for the complex hierarchical models as shown in
\par
[12]. To escape local maxima in learning, Elida et al. [13] propose a
\par
solution by perturbing training data. Two basic techniques are used
\par
to perturb the weights of the training data: (1) random reweighing,
\par
which randomly samples weight profiles on the training data, and
\par
(2) adversarial reweighing, which updates the weight profiles to ex-
\par
plicitly punish the current hypothesis, with the intent of moving the
\par
search quickly to a nearby basin of attraction. Although it usually
\par
achieves better solutions than EM, it is still a heuristic method and
\par
not necessarily able to escape local maxima. And also, it is much
\par
slower than the standard EM algorithm.
\par
The previous methods emphasize improving the machine learn-
\par
ing techniques, instead of using domain knowledge to help learning.
\par
Since there are no data available for hidden nodes, it is important to
\par
incorporate any available information about these nodes into learn-
\par
ing. The methods for constraining the parameters for a BN include
\par
Dirichlet priors, parameter sharing, and qualitative constraints. Ac-
\par
cording to [18], there are several problems using Dirichlet priors.
\par
First, it is impossible to represent even the simple equality con-
\par
straints on the parameters. Second, it is often beyond expert's capa-
\par
bility to specify a full Dirichlet prior over the parameters of a Bayesian
\par
network. Parameter sharing, on the other hand, allows parameters
\par
of different models to share the same values, i.e., it allows to impose
\par
equality constraints. Parameter sharing methods, however, do not
\par
capture more complicated constraints among parameters such as in-
\par
equality constraints among the parameters. In addition, both Dirich-
\par
let priors and parameter sharing methods are restricted to sharing
\par
parameters at the level of sharing a whole CPT or CPTs, instead of at
\par
the level of granularity of individual parameters. To overcome these
\par
limitations, others [19--22,18] propose to explicitly exploit qualita-
\par
tive relationships among parameters and systematically incorporate
\par
them into the parameter estimation process.
\par
Druzdel et al. [19] give formal definitions of several types of qual-
\par
itative relationships that can hold between nodes in a BN to help
\par
specify CPTs of BNs, including probability intervals, qualitative in-
\par
fluences, and qualitative synergies. They express these available in-
\par
formation in a canonical form consisting of (in)equalities expressing
\par
constraints on the hyperspace of possible joint probability distribu-
\par
tions, and then use this canonical form to derive upper and lower
\par
bounds on any probability of interest. However, the upper and lower
\par
bounds cannot give sufficient insight into how likely a value from
\par
the interval is to be the actual probability.
\par
Wittig and Jameson [20] present a method for integrating formal
\par
statements of qualitative constraints into two learning algorithms,
\par
APN [23,24] and EM. Two types of qualitative influences [19] are con-
\par
sidered as constraints for parameters during learning in this method:
\par
(1) a positive influence holds between two variables (X1, X2) if for
\par
any given value of X2, an increase in the value of X1 will not decrease
\par
the probability that the value of X2 is equal to or greater than that
\par
given value; and (2) a negative influence can be defined analogously.3048 W. Liao, Q. Ji / Pattern Recognition 42 (2009) 3046 -- 3056
\par
This paper shows that the accuracy of the learned BNs is superior to
\par
that of the corresponding BNs learned without the constraints.
\par
Even when data are complete, domain knowledge can help learn-
\par
ing significantly, in particular when insufficient data are available.
\par
Altendorf et al. [21] show how to interpret knowledge of qualita-
\par
tive influences, and in particular of monotonicities, as constraints on
\par
probability distribution, and to incorporate this knowledge into BN
\par
learning algorithms. It assumes that the values of the variables can be
\par
totally ordered. It focuses on learning from complete but sparse data.
\par
It shows the additional constraints provided by qualitative mono-
\par
tonicities can improve the performance of BN classifiers, particularly
\par
on extremely small training sets. The assumption that the values of
\par
the variables can be totally ordered is, however, too restrictive.
\par
Feelders and Gaag [22,25] present a method for learning BN pa-
\par
rameters with prior knowledge about the signs of influences between
\par
variables. The various signs are translated into order constraints on
\par
the network parameters and isotonic regression is then used to com-
\par
pute order-constrained estimates from the available data. They also
\par
focus only on learning from complete but sparse data. In addition,
\par
they assume all variables are binary.
\par
The constraints analyzed in these papers [20--22] are somewhat
\par
restrictive, in the sense that each constraint must involve all parame-
\par
ters in a conditional probability table (CPT). Niculescu [18] considers
\par
a different type of domain knowledge for constraining parameter es-
\par
timate when data are complete but limited. Two types of constraints
\par
are used: one is that the sum of several parameters within one con-
\par
ditional probability distribution is bounded by the sum of other pa-
\par
rameters in the same distribution; another is an upper bound on the
\par
sum of several parameters in one conditional probability distribution.
\par
Both the constraints have to be twice differentiable, with continu-
\par
ous second derivatives. They formalize the learning as a constraint
\par
optimization problem and derive closed form maximum likelihood
\par
parameter estimators.
\par
Our learning method also exploits domain knowledge to help pa-
\par
rameter learning, but especially for BNs with hidden nodes or a large
\par
amount of missing data. Different from other learning methods, the
\par
domain knowledge we used has the following features: (1) it does
\par
not need to involve all the parameters in a conditional probability
\par
table; (2) it can deal with relationships between different parame-
\par
ters; (3) it is associated with confidence levels to reflect the confi-
\par
dence of the domain experts; (4) it is easy to assess and define; and
\par
(5) new format of domain knowledge can be easily incorporated into
\par
the algorithm. Our algorithm systematically incorporates the domain
\par
knowledge and is capable of learning parameters successfully even
\par
when a large percent of nodes are hidden in a Bayesian network.
\par
