of facial expressions through the combination of only a small set of
\par
AUs.
\par
Current techniques for recognizing AUs are mostly based on com-
\par
puter vision techniques. But due to the richness, ambiguity, and dy-
\par
namic nature of facial actions, as well as the image uncertainty and
\par
individual difference, it is difficult to recognize each AU individually
\par
by using only computer vision techniques. Computer vision tech-
\par
niques should be combined with additional sematic information to
\par
achieve a more robust and consistent AU recognition. Fortunately,
\par
there are some inherent relationships among AUs as described in
\par
the FACS manual [26]. For example, the alternative rules provided in
\par
the FACS manual describe the mutual exclusive relationship among
\par
AUs. Furthermore, the FACS also includes the co-occurrence rules in
\par
their old version, which were ``designed to make scoring more de-
\par
terministic and conservative, and more reliable'' [26].
\par
Therefore, instead of recognizing each AU alone, a Bayesian net-
\par
work can be used to model the probabilistic relationships among
\par
different AUs, and given the BN model, AU recognition can be per-
\par
formed through a probabilistic inference [27]. Fig. 6 illustrates such
\par
a BN to model 14 AUs, and Table 3 summarizes a list of the 14 AUs
\par
and their meanings. They are adapted from [27]. Such a model is
\par
capable of representing the relationships among different AUs in a
\par
coherent and unified hierarchical structure, accounting for uncer-
\par
tainties in the AU recognition process with conditional dependence
\par
links, and providing principled inference and learning techniques to
\par
systematically combine the domain information and statistical in-
\par
formation extracted from the data.
\par
To incorporate the AU recognition results from a computer vision
\par
technique, in the BN model, each AU is connected with a measure-
\par
ment node (unshaded node), which encodes the measurement ob-
\par
tained from computer vision techniques. For AU measurement, we
\par
employ a technique similar to the one described in [28]. The output
\par
of the technique is a score for each AU, which is subsequently dis-
\par
credited to produce a value for a corresponding AU measurement
\par
node.
\par
5.2. AU model parameter learning
\par
Given the BN structure in Fig. 6 and the AU measurements, we
\par
then need parameterize the BN model before AU inference can com-
\par
mence. For this, we need training data. As defined before, a com-
\par
plete training sample requires the true AU label for each AU node
\par
and the measurement for each meresman node. However, manually
\par
labeling AUs is usually time consuming and difficult. Moreover, the
\par
labeling process is highly subjective, therefore prone to human er-
\par
rors. In addition, some AU events rarely happen in the collected data.
\par
Therefore, the training data could be incomplete, biased, or spare for
\par
certain AUs. We thus apply our algorithm to learn the BN parame-
\par
ters using only the AU measurements and some domain knowledge,
\par
without any AU labeling.
\par
We first generate constraints. For type I constraints, domain ex-
\par
perts are consulated to specify the approximate ranges for most of
\par
the parameters. Type II constraints are also constructed from domain
\par
specific knowledge. Specifically, for each measurement node, since
\par
the measurement accuracy for each AU varies, depending the com-
\par
puter vision technique used as well as on the difficulty of the AU,
\par
we can rank the measurements by their accuracy and then trans-
\par
late such a ranking into the outer-relationships between the cor-
\par
responding measurement nodes. For example, the computer vision
\par
technique usually performs better in recognition of AU2 (outer brow
\par
raiser) than AU23 (lip tighten), hence we can get constraints like
\par
p(O2=0\textbar AU2=0) {$>$} p(O23=0\textbar AU23=0), p(O2=1\textbar AU2=1) {$>$} p(O23=
\par
1\textbar AU23= 1), where 0 means an AU is absent, and 1 means an AU is
\par
present.
\par
More type-II constraints can be obtained based on the properties
\par
of different AUs. For example, for AU6 (cheek raiser) and AU12 (lip
\par
corner puller), the probability of AU12 being absent if AU6 is ab-
\par
sent, is smaller than the probability of AU6 being present if AU12
\par
is present, i.e., p(AU12= 0\textbar AU6= 0) {$<$} p(AU12= 1\textbar AU6= 1). For AU1
\par
(inner brow raiser), the influence of AU2 (outer brow raiser) on AU1
\par
is larger than the influence of AU5 (upper lid raiser) on AU1, i.e.
\par
p(AU1= 1\textbar AU2= 1, AU5= 0) {$>$} p(AU1= 1\textbar AU2= 0, AU5= 1). Overall,
