\textbf{1 Introduction}
\par
Product lifecycle management PLM activities involve the cre-
\par
ation and central management of all product data, along with the
\par
technologies used to access this information and knowledge, over
\par
the lifecycle of the product. During these activities, the product
\par
data generated in CAD/CAE applications regularly needs to be
\par
exchanged and shared between various applications. However, the
\par
many heterogeneous data representations used by different appli-
\par
cations result in an interoperability problem---these applications
\par
cannot understand and communicate with each other due to the
\par
lack of standard exchange format to communicate product defini-
\par
tion and lifecycle data. For example, a design feature is an inter-
\par
pretation based on the knowledge of a 3D feature-based CAD
\par
application for a product. This is not necessarily meaningful to an
\par
analysis application that is based on the concept of mesh ele-
\par
ments, although both sets of applications are referring to the same
\par
product. These are regarded as critical problems in PLM today
\par
and need to be addressed and automated.
\par
The context of the data is important and applications can pro-
\par
vide the context of the use of the data. The semantics of the data
\par
can be determined ``from the real-world applications that use the
\par
data'' 1 . Semantics is defined as the ``meaning and the use of
\par
data'' 2 . Semantics is all pervasive and covers many aspects such
\par
as the interpretation and the user perspective of the data 3 . Se-
\par
mantics is sometimes viewed primarily in terms of capturing the
\par
similarities between objects while others consider the identifica-
\par
tion of the relationships between objects as the key. One of the
\par
problems is that the types of applications have exploded 4 .
\par
Focusing on the product data in engineering applications, the
\par
following important roadblocks have been identified and are spe-
\par
cifically addressed in our work: 1 the need for an adequate prod-
\par
uct knowledge representation of product data, which is easily ex-
\par
pandable and customizable for both traditional e.g., CAD and
\par
nontraditional e.g., virtual assembly design information applica-
\par
tions; 2 the need for a way to use product engineering knowl-
\par
edge to interpret the actual product data; and 3 the need to find
\par
underlying similarities between different knowledge representa-
\par
tions and to use these similarities to map interpretations of the
\par
product data corresponding to different applications.
\par
We propose an ontology-based approach for a semantic level
\par
exchange and integration to improve interoperability. An ontology
\par
is an explicit specification of concepts in a domain. A common
\par
ontology defines the vocabulary with which queries and assertions
\par
are exchanged 5 . We provide a detailed description of our ap-
\par
proach and then present a simple prototype that allows semantic
\par
level exchange between two applications. The ultimate goal of
\par
this work is to allow support for complex design evaluations;
\par
however, that is not the specific focus of this paper.
\par
\textbf{2 Background and Literature Review}
\par
We present this section in three parts. We first discuss the com-
\par
mon approaches to product data representation and exchange stan-
\par
dards. We then discuss the methods that have been used to capture
\par
product semantic information. The final section introduces repre-
\par
sentative works in ontologies, specifically design ontology, sup-
\par
porting technologies for developing ontologies, and current ontol-
\par
ogy mapping approaches.
