\textbf{3 Proposed Approach for the Semantic Integration of}
\par
\textbf{Product Data in Engineering Applications}
\par
As discussed above, a product is considered with different
\par
points of views in different applications, so different representa-
\par
tions and terminologies may be used. For example, consider an
\par
automobile interior design evaluation. For the design of the dash-
\par
board, one knowledge base may focus on ergonomic comfort and
\par
accommodation, while another may focus on assembly simula-
\par
tion. The representation of the product, including data types, data
\par
structures, and terminology being used, vary greatly despite the
\par
fact that the underlying product is the same. Hence, it is desirable
\par
to explicitly build the knowledge representations to interpret the
\par
product data as required and also to help these representations
\par
exchange information.
\par
Our approach is to create an ontology based framework with
\par
ontology building, ontology mapping, and custom tools to support
\par
sharing and exchange of product and engineering data. We coin
\par
our overall approach as ontology-based adaptive design evalua-
\par
tion OADE Fig. 1 . The following are the key elements:
\par
a \emph{Design of knowledge representation}. Layered ontologies
\par
consisting of general domain, domain specific, and appli-
\par
cation specific ontologies to represent aspects of the en-
\par
gineering design knowledge. Three kind of
\par
relationships---composition, inheritance, and object-
\par
attribute---and related axioms define the restrictions on
\par
the concepts and relations between these concepts
\par
b \emph{Instantiation of source product data semantic concepts}.
\par
Instantiation of the source product data corresponding to
\par
the source knowledge representation ontology.
\par
c \emph{Creation of ontology mapping methods and mapping rep-}
\par
\emph{resentations}. Algorithms to systematically determine the
\par
corresponding mappings from the source to the target
\par
