\textbf{4.3 Basic Design Entity. }A design process can be considered
\par
as the process of collecting and creating information related to
\par
product data where the information can be divided into many
\par
units; each unit can have its own attribute and can be independent
\par
of the other units. We define the basic element of design informa-
\par
tion, which an engineer uses to describe a product design, as a
\par
BDE. A BDE has the following characteristics:
\par
{$\bullet$} A BDE is used as a basic information unit during design/
\par
analysis.
\par
{$\bullet$} A BDE normally has three basic attributes: form, behavior,
\par
and function. A BDE should, at the least, have a form at-
\par
tribute that relates to the geometry.
\par
{$\bullet$} A BDE is independent of other BDEs, but there are nor-
\par
mally interactions between them.
\par
Examples of BDE include the \emph{Feature }concept in 3D feature-
\par
based design and feature-based manufacturing, and the concepts
\par
of \emph{part}, \emph{assembly}, and \emph{joint }in assembly design/simulation. As can
\par
be seen, in different design/analysis domains, design entities could
\par
differ and have different attribute definitions.
\par
\textbf{4.4 Attributes of a Basic Design Entity. }To describe basic
\par
attributes of a BDE, form-behavior-function triplet concepts are
\par
used. All attributes of a basic design entity are categorized as
\par
follows: 1 form attribute, 2 behavior attribute, and 3 function
\par
attribute. In addition, we seek to include not just the concepts that
\par
can be used to represent the attributes of the final product but also
\par
to describe intermediate information of the importance generated
\par
during the product design/analysis process. Examples include in-
\par
formation generated during the design process such as the way a
\par
product was created, the creation/manufacturing order, etc. This is
\par
made possible by extending the form, behavior, and function at-
\par
tributes. We consider only the form and behavior attributes in this
\par
work.
\par
\emph{4.4.1 Form Attribute}. Form attribute includes information
\par
about the design such as the material, geometry, etc. In this paper
\par
