\par
haviors that occur during the design process, which can be used to
\par
present information such as the design intent and history. Under
\par
design behaviors, we identified the following two categories: 1
\par
design constraint behavior, such as parametric constraint, to de-
\par
scribe the mathematical relationships between parameters, and 2
\par
Design creation behavior, such as parametric feature creation, to
\par
describe the way a 3D feature is created. For example, in PRO/
\par
ENGINEER, a 3D feature is usually created by a design creation
\par
behavior using a 2D geometry profile. However, a feature such as
\par
a cylinder can be created in one of two ways: 1 a rectangle
\par
profile revolving around an axis, or 2 a circle profile extruded in
\par
a direction normal to its profile, as depicted in Fig. 4.
\par
Various behaviors focus on the different aspects of the product
\par
design, which are defined and used across engineering design/
\par
analysis domains. In order to find similar behavior attributes that
\par
are shared by both of the applications during ontology mapping,
\par
common behavior attributes between engineering design and
\par
analysis applications need to be identified. Consider the following
\par
examples:
\par
1 If both applications are in the domain of the 3D feature-
\par
based product design, such as PRO/ENGINEER, SOLIDWORKS,
\par
or CATIA, then \emph{designCreationBehavior }can be used as a
\par
common behavior attribute.
\par
2 If both applications share a common definition in \emph{design-}
\par
\emph{ConstraintBehavior}, then that definition can be used as a
\par
behavior attribute. For example, in the product design and
