\emph{\_}of property. Also, \emph{has\_feature}/\emph{is\_feature\_of }is inher-
\par
ited from has\_part/part\_of property to indicate the relation be-
\par
tween the two concepts of feature and component.
\par
Transitivity is an important property used in logical reasoning
\par
62 . In our work, we define two transitivity rules between the
\par
relations. These are used in the mapping rules that are described in
\par
Secs. 4.5.1 and 4.5.2.
\par
\emph{4.5.1 Relations Transitivity Rule 1: Transitivity Between At-}
\par
\emph{tribute\_of and Part\_of Relations}. We define that the attribute\_of
\par
relation is transitive over the part\_of relation. This means that if A
\par
has a part B, and B has an attribute C, then A will have the
\par
attribute C. For example, according to the rule, if a part has\_fea-
\par
ture introduced in Sec.4.5 as a property inherited from has\emph{\_}part
\par
property extrude, and the extrude has a geometry attribute of a
\par
circular profile, then the part also has the geometry attribute of a
\par
circular profile. A rule is defined:
\par
RULE DEFINITION. \emph{For three concepts C1, C2, and C3, if C1}
\par
\emph{has\_part C2 and C2 has\_Attribute C3, then it can be concluded}
\par
\emph{that C1 has\_Attribute C3.}
\par
\emph{4.5.2 Relations Transitivity Rule 2: Transitivities of the At-}
\par
\emph{tribute\_of and Part\_of Relations Over the Is\_a Relation}. We de-
\par
fine that the attribute\_of and part\_of relations are transitive over
\par
the is\_a relation. For example, according to the rule, if an auto-
\par
mobile has\_part of tire and a truck \emph{is a kind of }an automobile, then
\par
truck also has\_part tire. A rule is defined:
\par
RULE DEFINITION\emph{. For three concepts C1, C2, and C3, if C1}
\par
\emph{has\_attribute C2, or C1 has\_part C2, and C3 is derived from C1,}
\par
\emph{then it can be concluded that C3 has\_attribute C2 or C3 has\_part}
\par
\emph{C2.}
\par
\textbf{5 Instantiation of the Source Product Data Semantics}
\par
\textbf{Concepts to the Build Instance Layer}
\par
Product data semantics refers to the ``meaning'' of the product
\par
data. After the concepts are built into the engineering ontologies,
\par
concepts need to be instantiated with the data from the actual
\par
product so that product data semantics are generated by the asso-
\par
ciation of each product data to its concept level. To capture prod-
\par
\textbf{Journal of Computing and Information Science in Engineering }uct data semantics based on the predefined concepts and their
\par
relations in engineering ontology, instances are created to repre-
\par
sent the product data. Since most of the commercial CAD/CAE
\par
applications provide open API to access information generated in
\par
the application, information about the product can be obtained by
\par
calling the API. Instances in the application specific ontologies are
\par
built based on this information.
\par
