\textbf{8 Future Work}
\par
The solution in this paper addresses some key issues related to
\par
knowledge representation and mapping between engineering on-
\par
tologies. However, there are still several problems which are be-
\par
ing addressed and further investigated.
\par
{$\bullet$} The three layered engineering ontology structure needs to be
\par
supplemented with detailed and relevant concepts in the
\par
general domain, domain specific, and application specific
\par
ontologies.
\par
{$\bullet$} Mapping methods are being refined, and a bridge ontology
\par
is being designed.
\par
{$\bullet$} We need to further expand and refine the domain specific
\par
ontologies for more domains.
\par
{$\bullet$} Currently the application specific layer supports only one
\par
CAD system. It can be extended to other CAD/CAE sys-
\par
tems.
\par
{$\bullet$} More complex and practical examples are being tested.
\par
{$\bullet$} This work is being extended to more substantially support
\par
design evaluations.
\par
{$\bullet$} Inference support is another key capability that is being
\par
added.
\par
{$\bullet$} Tests are being conducted to document the advantage of
\par
using this ontology approach versus other traditional meth-
\par
ods such as object oriented methods, UML, database tech-
\par
nologies, etc.
\par
\textbf{9 Summary}
\par
Some of the interoperability problems in design evaluations can
\par
be resolved through the use of ontologies for knowledge represen-
\par
tation and mapping methods to automatically translate the inter-
\par
pretations between them. Our solution is to explicitly build into
\par
ontologies the engineering product data knowledge, which is used
\par
in different design and analysis processes. We automatically trans-
\par
late the interpretation from one representation to another such that
\par
different applications in the integration framework can understand
\par
each other. With layer-structured ontologies coded in a consistent,
\par
scalable ontology language, new ontologies can be built based on
\par
existing ontologies so as to improve the scalability and reusability
\par
of the framework. By mapping to other ontologies, an interpreta-
\par
tion of data based on one knowledge representation can be trans-
\par
lated into other interpretations based on other knowledge repre-
\par
sentations. Our ongoing research is seeking to further
\par
improvements on semantic level interoperability and integration
\par
for design evaluations.
